## Outcome

Treat Actions Runtime itself as the **lightweight, local-first Control Room** for capabilities and execution, then replace the current Robot-specific producer/consumer modal with one capability-aware **Run Composer** and one durable Run experience.

The product should make it obvious that Actions Runtime is more than an HTTP wrapper around `@action` functions:

- it catalogs Actions, Robot Tasks, Workflows, and development capabilities;
- it resolves runtime adapters and environments;
- it preflights bindings, inputs, workers, and policy;
- it creates/observes/cancels Runs;
- it surfaces logs, artifacts, Work Items, schedules, triggers, workers, and diagnostics;
- it remains useful as one bundled local binary while the same semantics can expand to shared/distributed deployments.

The goal is not to rebuild the historical Robocorp or `yorko-io/control-room` product. It is to bring the **right control-room concepts** into the already-existing Actions Runtime shell with fewer moving parts and open adapter/provider contracts.

## Product thesis

```text
Actions Runtime
  = capability catalog
  + package/task management
  + runtime adapter/environment control
  + Run/Attempt control and evidence
  + optional Work Items/workflows/schedules/triggers
  + worker/provider diagnostics
```

RCC is bundled and shown as the first rich runtime adapter, including its Environment Artifact/provider/cache capabilities. It is not hidden, reimplemented, or presented as the only possible backend.

A uv, raw Python, direct Robot Framework, or future adapter appears through the same #143 product contract with its own truthful capabilities and limitations.

## Control Room archaeology: retain the domain, reject the sprawl

The archived `yorko-io/control-room` backend was trying to solve a real domain much broader than its frontend screenshots:

- Task Packages and Tasks;
- Processes, ordered Steps, dependencies, and process/step Runs;
- worker groups, self-hosted workers, on-demand runtimes, provisioning, and heartbeats;
- Holotree catalogs/environments;
- environment variables, Vault secrets, desktop connection, priority, timeout, and parallelism;
- Work Item queues and state;
- live logs, artifacts, S3 publication, metrics, and analytics;
- schedules, triggers, webhooks, notifications, and callbacks;
- orphan recovery, graceful shutdown, retention, quotas, and audit.

Those are useful clues about what users need from an automation control plane.

The implementation also shows what not to copy:

- separate RCC and Holotree control-plane services duplicating RCC internals;
- Redis/Kubernetes/service decomposition before durable local semantics were solid;
- package/runtime identity based on local paths;
- Work Item and capability inference from names/source scans;
- synthetic jobs/Work Items for every Robot;
- simulated success when RCC, worker deployment, or registration failed;
- one giant backend owning package distribution, environment construction, worker provisioning, process orchestration, and business state.

Actions Runtime should absorb the domain model incrementally while keeping clear ownership:

| Control Room concept | Actions Runtime/open contract |
|---|---|
| Task Package | Package Revision/development generation |
| Task | Robot Task capability |
| Process | Workflow definition/run (#141) |
| Process Step | Workflow node / child capability Run |
| Runtime Environment | Runtime Adapter + Worker Profile/placement |
| Holotree environment page | RCC adapter diagnostics from #133/#143 |
| Worker Group | Worker Profile/pool (#90/#138) |
| Vault/config | Deployment bindings/providers (#87/#129) |
| Work Item queue | #132 business queue contract |
| Step/Process Run | common Run/Attempt/lineage |
| Output files/logs | #86 artifacts/results + #137 evidence |
| On-demand runtime | execution-placement adapter, not a required K8s product |

