- pre-run scripts under policy;
- artifacts directory;
- optional Work Item context;
- Dev Tasks excluded from ordinary production execution.

### Other adapters

Consume explicit Package v3/compatibility declarations and adapter-specific normalized metadata. Do not infer compatibility from filenames or fall back to host shell.

Every placement fetches/verifies the exact Package Revision through #136 instead of requiring the original Runtime checkout.

## Work Items

Work Items remain optional and explicit through #143/#132.

The same matrix must include:

- one standalone no-Work-Item task;
- one optional Work Item task run both ways;
- one queue/consumer scenario where declared;
- no task-name/source-import inference;
- no generated synthetic Work Item merely because a Robot exists.

## Action to Robot invocation

#147 owns parent/child Run semantics.

The parent should only need capability-level intent, conceptually:

```python
runtime.invoke(
    robot_task_ref,
    input=...,
    worker_profile="private-vpc-browser",
)
```

Moving the child between local/container/independent placements—or deliberately selecting another compatible runtime for a new Run—must not require parent code to call RCC, uv, Robot Framework, Docker, ECS, SSH, or Kubernetes directly.

## Secrets and bindings

- resolve through #87/#129 at the selected execution boundary;
- never put plaintext values in source/package/runtime artifacts, durable input, command line, image metadata, labels, logs, events, or output artifacts;
- use narrow/short-lived grants where possible;
- register redaction before child output starts;
- never bake Deployment secrets into reusable images or runtime artifacts;
- worker loss/retry follows pinned binding/version policy;
- a different runtime adapter cannot gain broader grants merely by being selected.

## Common Run/control behavior

All matrix cells use:

- one logical Run;
- fenced Attempts;
- explicit runtime/placement identities;
- queued/preparing/running/terminal evidence;
- bounded logs/progress;
- cancellation escalation and full cleanup;
- worker/adapter loss, retry, and ambiguous-effect policy;
- artifact/result publication;
- parent/child lineage;
- #85 MCP Task projection;
- #137 evidence/telemetry.

A Docker API response, RCC/uv/Robot process exit, cloud CLI, cache server, or health endpoint is not authoritative Run state.

No adapter or placement may simulate success when unavailable.

