## Outcome

Define a bounded durable workflow/recipe model for capability sequences whose ordering, Runtime Plan selection, retries, effects, Work Item transitions, fan-out/fan-in, and human review cannot live only in an LLM prompt, agent transcript, process memory, or backend-specific shell script.

An external agent may select a workflow, supply inputs, reason inside declared steps, or react to outputs. Actions Runtime owns deterministic workflow state and child Run semantics; it does not become a general agent reasoning harness.

This is the open lightweight-Control-Room successor to the useful Process→Steps→Tasks model, built on common Package/Capability/Runtime Plan/Run/Work Item contracts rather than a separate Robot process engine.

## Motivation

Real RPA/agent packages often express:

```text
retrieve input / Work Item
  -> extract
  -> transform
  -> validate
  -> branch or review
  -> reconcile / mutate
  -> generate artifacts
  -> complete / fail / compensate
```

A model can provide judgment, but it should not be the only durable authority for:

- required ordering and conditions;
- which immutable capability/runtime implementation ran;
- retry/backoff and no-fallback behavior;
- idempotency/effect receipts;
- compensation;
- Work Item ownership/business state;
- review persistence;
- crash recovery;
- update/rollback pinning;
- artifacts/result lineage;
- worker/adapter/placement eligibility.

## Relationship to historical Control Room

Preserve these useful concepts:

- Process definition and Process Run;
- ordered named Steps;
- each Step targets one Task/capability;
- per-step inputs/config/worker/runtime/timeout/parallelism/priority;
- Work Item forwarding/dependencies;
- schedules/triggers/notifications;
- step-level Runs/logs/artifacts/errors.

Map them to common objects:

| Historical concept | Actions Runtime contract |
|---|---|
| Process | Workflow Definition |
| Process Run | Workflow Run |
| Step | Workflow Node |
| Task Package / Task | Package Revision / Capability |
| runtime environment | selected Runtime Plan/Adapter |
| worker group/runtime | Worker Profile/placement |
| Work Item forwarding | explicit typed Work Item/data mapping |
| step execution | child capability Run/Attempt |

Do not copy backend-specific RCC path resolution, Kubernetes runtime provisioning, synthetic Work Item generation, name heuristics, or simulation.

## Workflow object model

### Workflow Definition

Immutable Package Revision-scoped declaration containing:

- workflow ID/version/schema;
- typed inputs/outputs;
- nodes and dependencies;
- selected package capability IDs rather than mutable names/URLs;
- Runtime Plan policy per node:
  - pinned plan declared in the same Package Revision;
  - Deployment-selected default/allowed plans;
  - explicit authorized override rules;
- deterministic edges/conditions;
- input/output/artifact/Work Item mappings;
- timeout/retry/cancellation/effect policy;
- fan-out/fan-in/join policy;
- interaction/review points;
- compensation/manual-resolution policy;
- required providers/Worker Profiles/placements;
- concurrency/priority/backpressure policy;
- compatibility/migration metadata.

A node never embeds raw RCC/uv/Robot Framework/Docker/Kubernetes command lines, local paths, provider URLs, or sockets as workflow authority.

### Workflow Run

One durable execution pinned to:

- Workspace/Deployment Revision;
- Package Revision/Workflow Definition;
- workflow-level binding/policy snapshot;
- initiating actor/event/Work Item;
- immutable normalized input;
- shared admission schema/mode/generation;
- current durable node state and lineage;
- deadline/max age/cancellation intent.

A Workflow Run creates ordinary child capability Runs. Do not make one worker process, adapter service, model transcript, or browser session workflow authority.

### Node Run

Durable causal record linking one Workflow Node to:

- resolved capability identity;
- selected/pinned Runtime Plan/`runtime_kind`;
- binding/Worker Profile/placement snapshot;
- one logical child Run and its Attempts, or a bounded fan-out set;
- interaction/review record;
- provider operation/effect receipt;
- artifact/result/Work Item transitions;
- retry/compensation lineage;
- terminal node outcome.

Node Run identity/outcome survives Attempt retry. Retry never selects a different Runtime Plan. Deliberate recovery under another adapter is a new child Run/manual policy decision with explicit evidence.

## Shared admission

Before a node becomes executable, resolve and consume #143's canonical AdmissionSnapshot for the exact:

- Workflow Definition/Run generation;
- node/capability;
- selected Runtime Plan;
- normalized mapped input;
- binding snapshot;
- Worker Profile/placement;
- policy generation.

Use the same off/shadow/enforce mode applicator as UI/CLI/direct Runs.

- shadow records blockers/unknown/partial evidence without changing workflow decisions;
- enforce gates only enrolled nodes/workflows;
- mode/generation captured once per scheduling pass/node decision;
- no duplicated workflow-specific Ready()/DependsOn logic;
- Run creation revalidates immutable identity/auth/freshness;
- adapter outage never triggers fallback.

## Execution semantics

Start with a deliberately small deterministic model:

- sequential nodes;
- conditional branches over typed prior outputs/state;
- bounded parallel fan-out;
- explicit join `all`/`any`/quorum where justified;
- subworkflow invocation only with depth/recursion/version rules;
- deterministic data mapping, not hidden prompt interpolation;
- durable pause for interaction/Needs Review;
- cancellation propagation;
- retry/backoff by failure class;
- deadlines/max workflow age;
- terminal success/business failure/application failure/cancel/ambiguous/manual-review outcomes.

Do not start with general BPMN, arbitrary distributed DAG language, or arbitrary Python control code inside coordinator.

## Runtime Plan and adapter rules

Each child node Run pins one exact Runtime Plan from Package/Deployment policy.

- RCC nodes use #133 Environment Artifact/provider/lease/exec semantics;
- uv/Python/Robot Framework nodes use their own namespaced plans;
- a Workflow Definition may contain nodes using different adapters deliberately;
- the workflow engine reasons only over declared capability/result/outcome contracts, not adapter internals;
- retry preserves adapter/plan;
- an installed compatible alternative is not fallback;
- adapter unavailable/auth-required/incompatible/no-worker are explicit blocked/failure states according to policy;
- package code cannot select arbitrary adapter plugins, images, sockets, mounts, or providers;
- placement and runtime adapter remain separate.

Example:

```text
Node 1: Action Normalize        runtime=rcc      placement=local/worker
Node 2: Robot Investigate      runtime=rcc      placement=private-browser
Node 3: Robot Framework Verify runtime=robot-fw placement=windows-desktop
Node 4: Action Record Result    runtime=uv       placement=ordinary-worker
```

Such heterogeneity is declared/pinned, never opportunistic.

## Model-assisted nodes

A workflow may invoke an Action that calls a model or pause for external agent/human judgment.

Represent model judgment as explicit capability/node with:

- typed input/output;
- provider/secret/network policy;
- selected Runtime Plan/Worker Profile;
- timeout/retry/cost policy;
- confidence/escalation semantics owned by that capability;
- evidence/artifact references;
- no assumption that transcript is durable workflow state.

The coordinator deterministically maps declared outcomes to transitions.

## State, data, and artifacts

- inline workflow state bounded/versioned/typed;
- large files/tables/logs through #86/#131 handles;
- secrets/provider credentials resolved at child Attempt, never stored in workflow state;
- node outputs record exact schema/Package/Runtime Plan and immutable references;
- mapping failures are explicit workflow/application failures;
- adapter-specific preparation evidence stays namespaced on child Runs/Attempts;
- retention/GC follows Workflow→Node→Run/Task/Work Item/artifact/runtime references.

## Side effects, idempotency, and compensation

Every consequential node declares one of:

- idempotent under stable workflow/node effect key;
- provider transaction where supported;
- durable effect receipt;
- explicit compensation capability;
- non-retryable after ambiguous execution and must enter review.

Exactly-once external effects are not assumed.

Changing runtime adapter is not an infrastructure retry because behavior/effect timing may differ. It requires a new child Run/decision and does not erase ambiguity.

Compensation is another pinned capability Run with its own Runtime Plan/evidence. Compensation failure/partial completion are explicit.

## Work Item integration

#132 owns business queue/review state.

A Workflow may be initiated by a Work Item and create outputs. Define:

- fenced claim/ownership relationship during long workflow;
- whether Work Item lease is renewed, released/paused, or transitioned while waiting;
- current owner authority for final state/output commit;
- Needs Review mapping;
- schedule/not-before;
- parent/child/fan-out lineage;
- replay/restart behavior;
- typed Work Item forwarding/mapping between nodes;
- no automatic equivalence among Work Item, Workflow Run, child Run, Task, or Attempt state;
- no producer/consumer inference from names/source.

A standalone workflow or Robot node can run without Work Items when declared.

## Interaction and review

Use #92 durable Interaction/Review records for:

- approval/rejection;
- typed edits;
- evidence inspection;
- ambiguous effect resolution;
- authorized branch choice;
- manual complete/cancel/retry/restart-as-new.

Connected MCP interaction optimizes delivery; pause survives disconnect and restarts.

Approval binds exact Workflow/Deployment/Package/node Runtime Plan/protected inputs. Changing consequential fields or runtime plan invalidates approval.

## Definition publication/update

- Definition immutable inside Package Revision;
- Deployment projection decides external visibility and Runtime Plan policy;
- existing Workflow Runs remain pinned after Package/Deployment/adapter promotion/update;
- restart-as-new may resolve current revision/plans;
- retry/resume does not;
- old definitions/runtime plans/artifacts retained while referenced;
- active state-schema migration is not automatic;
- adapter revocation/update has explicit active-Run policy and never rewrites history.

## Scheduling and workers

Coordinator creates pinned child Runs through #83; #90/#138 schedule Attempts.

- no worker-local DAG authority;
- failover resumes from durable node state;
- package/runtime cache locality is optimization;
- fan-out obeys quotas/backpressure;
- no eligible worker/adapter yields explicit queued/blocked node state and Task, not held request;
- stale node/Attempt cannot advance workflow;
- nested parent/child capacity deadlock is prevented with #147 rules;
- worker/adaptor decline preserves the selected Runtime Plan.

## Triggers

Initial triggers may include:

- MCP/HTTP/CLI invocation;
- Work Item creation/claim;
- schedule/not-before;
- webhook/message/event adapter;
- parent workflow node.

Each normalizes to idempotent Workflow Run creation under Workspace/Deployment/admission authorization. Broker/event delivery is notification/input, not workflow state authority.

## Observability and conformance

#137/#139 evidence chain:

```text
trigger / Work Item
  -> Workflow Run
    -> Node Run + admission snapshot
      -> child capability Run + Runtime Plan
        -> Attempt / adapter / worker / placement
          -> provider effects / artifacts / interactions / output Work Items
```

Expose state, blocked reason, critical path, progress, retries, adapter/runtime preparation, review age, and compact failure without embedding bulk logs/data.

Conformance includes:

- RCC and one non-RCC node;
- mixed adapters in one workflow;
- selected adapter outage with compatible alternative installed—no fallback;
- off/shadow/enforce admission;
- exact-subject proof invalidation;
- worker/adapter retry and stale output;
- Work Item/no-Work-Item nodes;
- cancellation and compensation.

## Smallest vertical

Implement one remittance-like local workflow:

1. Work Item/document fixture input.
2. deterministic extraction/normalization Action under pinned Runtime Plan.
3. validation Action.
4. branch:
   - valid → reconciliation capability;
   - ambiguous → durable review.
5. reviewer approves/rejects/edits authorized fields.
6. reconciliation uses idempotency/effect receipt.
7. report published as artifact.
8. Work Item completes/fails through fenced transition.
9. source/Runtime Plan/Deployment update during pause does not redirect active workflow.
10. Runtime/worker restart resumes.
11. one node is a Robot Task; a later conformance variation uses a non-RCC plan without changing workflow engine semantics.

Run collapsed locally, then against independent workers.

## Failure matrix

- crash before/after child Run creation/link;
- duplicate trigger/idempotency;
- adapter preparation failure;
- compatible alternative present but no fallback;
- child retry/ambiguous effect;
- fan-out partial failure/join timeout;
- interaction disconnect/expiry/duplicate response;
- cancellation during queued/preparing/running/review/compensation;
- Work Item lease loss/reassignment;
- artifact publication failure;
- Deployment/Runtime Plan update/rollback mid-run;
- coordinator/database restart;
- stale worker/node output;
- compensation failure;
- nested capacity deadlock;
- admission mode/generation change between passes.

## Acceptance

1. workflow state survives Runtime/client/worker restart and is not transcript/process state.
2. every child Run pins Package/capability/Runtime Plan/bindings/profile.
3. retry never changes adapter or silently duplicates effects.
4. workflow can deliberately compose RCC and one non-RCC capability without generic RCC fields.
5. review survives disconnect and resumes exactly once.
6. Work Item outcome is fenced and causally linked.
7. large outputs stay in handles.
8. update/rollback/adapter promotion affect new Workflow Runs only.
9. fan-out/fan-in/cancellation deterministic under faults.
10. collapsed/distributed preserve logical result.
11. shared admission is the only execution gate and supports off/shadow/enforce.
12. external agents invoke/observe workflows without Runtime becoming an agent harness.

## Non-goals

- General BPMN.
- General agent planner/conversation memory.
- Arbitrary unbounded DAG/control code.
- Exactly-once effects by assumption.
- Replacing Work Items/Runs/Tasks/Interactions with one row.
- Requiring Kubernetes/broker/RCC daemon.
- Adapter fallback or backend-specific workflow authority.

