## Outcome

Define install, configuration, process roles, adapter/runtime-provider integration, health/readiness, upgrade, backup/restore, and disaster-recovery contracts for Actions Runtime in both collapsed local-first and split control/worker forms.

Semantic richness must not force an operational microservice maze. Actions Runtime should remain the lightweight Control Room for its own capabilities while composing RCC and other Runtime Adapters through explicit seams.

Do not require Kubernetes, Redis, broker, remote cache server, or one process per package to obtain correct semantics.

## Supported topology profiles

### Local development / personal control room

```text
one actions-runtime process
  synthesized/default Workspace
  SQLite
  local Package generations
  local filesystem output/provider roots
  bundled RCC adapter by default
  optional installed adapters
  local process worker/placement
  Runtime UI/CLI
```

No external service, container engine, login, provider, or worker registration required for ordinary local execution.

RCC local/offline Holotree works without `rcc cache serve`. Other adapters are optional and explicit.

### Simple production

```text
1..N Runtime gateway/coordinator processes
          |
      PostgreSQL
          |
1..N independent workers
  installed/authorized Runtime Adapters
  local package/runtime caches
          |
shared Package/output/secret/data/Work Item/runtime-artifact providers as configured
```

No broker/Kubernetes required if DB-backed claims/control suffice.

### Provider-backed RCC distribution

Optionally run RCC's own `rcc cache serve` or another supported RCC provider for immutable Environment Artifact distribution.

It remains separately owned RCC infrastructure:

- not the Runtime coordinator;
- not worker registration/scheduling;
- not Run/cancellation/fencing authority;
- not an Actions-owned duplicate service;
- not required for verified warm/local/offline execution.

### Strong isolation

Separate installations or dedicated Worker Profiles/pools/trust domains may isolate Workspaces, regulated/consequential work, untrusted packages, browser/desktop sessions, GPUs/devices, or private networks.

Single-Workspace-per-installation remains a first-class safe default. Multi-Workspace requires application/provider/adapter negative evidence.

## Distribution and process roles

Define supported commands/modes equivalent to:

- `actions-runtime dev` — collapsed authoring/autoreload;
- `actions-runtime serve` — protocol/catalog/Deployment/admission/Run coordinator;
- `actions-runtime worker` — independent worker/placement executor;
- `actions-runtime migrate` — database migration/status;
- `actions-runtime doctor` — adapter/provider/worker/package/runtime diagnostics;
- support/evidence export;
- optional repository-owned Package/output/provider service modes where justified.

Prefer one codebase/binary with role modes until independent distribution has demonstrated value.

Do not add an Actions `rcc-cache-server` clone. Use RCC's supported binary/service where configured.

Source, wheel, frozen binary, container image, and system-service distributions expose compatible capability/Runtime Plan/Run behavior and exact build provenance.

## Runtime Adapter packaging

### Built-in/default RCC adapter

Actions Runtime distributions should:

- bundle or securely locate/download a supported RCC release;
- verify checksum/provenance;
- negotiate required RCC features/version rather than health only;
- support explicit external RCC override for development/testing;
- support local/offline/provider-backed behavior;
- expose Environment Artifact/provider/cache/lease/exec diagnostics;
- avoid undocumented RCC commands;
- never simulate/fallback when RCC is unavailable.

RCC remains independently usable from its own CLI.

### Additional adapters

Adapters such as uv, Python, or Robot Framework are:

- installed/authorized by operator/distribution policy;
- versioned/provenanced;
- default-off/shadow before promotion;
- enabled for specific capability/platform/placement subsets only after #139 conformance;
- not loadable from untrusted package source;
- independently removable/revocable;
- never automatic fallback.

Define packaging for optional built-in modules versus external executables without creating an unrestricted plugin loader.

### Placement backends

Package local process, rootless container, Windows desktop/session, VM/sandbox, or future placement support independently from Runtime Adapters.

Not every adapter supports every placement. Capability image/rootfs identity remains distinct from Package/runtime artifact identity.

## Configuration model

Define typed ownership/precedence for:

- installation/Workspace identity;
- database;
- Package source, output, data, secret, Work Item, and adapter-runtime provider references;
- authentication/authorization;
- adapter installation/authorization/promotion/enrollment;
- admission off/shadow/enforce mode and boundary enrollment;
- Worker Profiles/registration/trust/capacity;
- RCC binary/home/provider profiles and approved cache-service endpoints;
- uv/Python/Robot Framework adapter settings;
- placement engines/images/resource/network/mount/device policy;
- proxy/CA/TLS/no-proxy;
- local cache/provider roots and quotas;
- telemetry/audit exporters;
- retention/GC;
- concurrency/timeouts/rate limits;
- compatibility/migration mode.

Secrets are provider references/secure local sources, never copied into Package/Deployment/Run state or printed.

Validation fails early, redacts values, distinguishes local convenience from production policy, and never silently falls back from configured shared/provider/adapter state.

## Health, readiness, admission, and proof

Separate liveness, readiness, compatibility, dynamic capacity, and real conformance.

### Runtime/coordinator

- process alive;
- database reachable/schema compatible/migrations complete;
- Deployment/catalog/Runtime Plan resolver ready;
- shared admission service/config/mode valid;
- auth policy valid;
- required provider capabilities available where needed;
- ability to create/observe/cancel Runs;
- no execution-ready claim when no eligible worker/adapter exists.

### Worker

- process alive;
- registered/authorized;
- worker protocol/schema compatibility;
- installed Runtime Adapter descriptors/features/provenance;
- placement backend descriptors;
- Package/runtime/output provider reachability where required;
- local cache/disk pressure;
- capacity/drain/degraded/auth-required state;
- exact fixture package/runtime acquisition and real probe execution under policy.

### Runtime Adapter

Distinguish:

- installed;
- descriptor readable;
- auth/config ready;
- compatible with a particular Runtime Plan;
- able to prepare required content;
- real conformance receipt for exact supported subset;
- currently degraded/unavailable.

`--version` or health endpoint is not execution readiness.

### Providers

Distinguish service liveness from protocol/version/trust/content closure/read/write capability.

For RCC, distinguish local warm content, provider reachability, required missing objects, and `cache serve` compatibility.

Liveness never triggers expensive builds. Readiness failures are actionable/redacted. Shared admission consumes these observations without duplicating health logic.

## Startup and shutdown

Define:

- migration order/startup coordination;
- DB/provider/adapter unavailable behavior;
- no fallback from PostgreSQL/shared provider to local state;
- no runtime-adapter substitution;
- worker registration/retry/backoff;
- adapter descriptor/feature negotiation;
- Runtime and worker graceful drain;
- no new Attempts after drain;
- cancellation/reconciliation of active Attempts;
- process/container/session cleanup;
- Package and adapter-local runtime lease release;
- provider upload completion/abort;
- bounded shutdown and forced-termination evidence;
- restart recovery from every durable/provisional state;
- orphan cleanup without falsifying Run outcome.

## Upgrade and rollback

Define rolling compatibility across:

- Runtime coordinator versions;
- worker protocol (#138);
- database schema (#84);
- Package/Deployment/Runtime Plan schemas (#129/#130/#143);
- adapter contract/implementation versions;
- RCC Environment Manifest/Object Index/catalog/builder/provider versions (#133);
- uv/Python/Robot Framework plan versions;
- placement backend versions/images;
- provider protocols;
- frontend/Canvas artifacts;
- admission schema/mode/generation.

Required behavior:

1. preflight/admission validates compatibility before rollout;
2. DB migrations coordinated/forward-compatible for declared window;
3. new/old Runtime and workers coexist or fail closed;
4. old/new Package/Runtime Plan/runtime artifacts remain available while Runs drain;
5. in-flight Run never changes adapter/plan during rollout;
6. rollback affects new resolution without rewriting history;
7. destructive changes require explicit maintenance plan;
8. downgrade cannot ignore fencing/auth/runtime/admission-critical fields;
9. release artifacts retain exact provenance/checksums;
10. newly added adapters remain shadow/default-off until exact-subject #139 promotion evidence exists.

Application rollback is not business-data rollback. Adapter rollback is not permission to retry a Run under another adapter.

## Backup, restore, and disaster recovery

Coordinate separately owned planes:

- PostgreSQL Runtime/Deployment/Run/admission/audit state;
- Package Revision provider;
- adapter-specific runtime artifact providers:
  - RCC Environment Artifacts/provider configuration;
  - uv/other immutable runtime content where applicable;
- output artifact/result store;
- Work Items/attachments;
- provider configuration/references/trust policy;
- adapter/worker/placement configuration/provenance;
- local caches as disposable/non-authoritative unless explicitly the only retained local artifact under a supported local profile.

Specify:

- consistency point/order;
- encryption/credential separation;
- profile-specific RPO/RTO;
- restore to same/new installation/Workspace;
- digest/closure verification;
- missing provider/content reconciliation;
- retention/legal hold;
- DR drill/evidence receipt;
- no backup of secret plaintext where provider references suffice;
- air-gapped restore with preseeded Package and selected runtime content;
- no reconstruction of historical Run identity from local paths.

For RCC, prefer RCC-supported export/import/provider/content verification. Do not scrape Holotree directories as an Actions backup format.

## Installation artifacts

Support:

- source/wheel/frozen binary;
- conventional system process/service;
- container image where useful;
- simple VM/Compose-like reference;
- air-gapped preseeded Package/runtime/provider artifacts;
- optional Helm/Kubernetes reference only after process semantics/need are proven.

Kubernetes/containers remain placement/install adapters and do not define Package/Run/runtime semantics.

## Resource planning and quotas

Controls for:

- Runtime replicas/DB connections;
- workers/adapter/placement concurrency;
- Package/runtime cache disk;
- provider bytes/objects;
- cold preparation/acquisition/prewarm storms;
- logs/artifacts/results;
- Work Item queues;
- per-Workspace/Deployment/Runtime Plan quotas;
- browser/desktop/GPU pools;
- overload admission/backpressure;
- provider/cache request limits.

No worker/capacity yields queued Run/Task rather than held protocol request. No capacity pressure triggers adapter fallback.

