## Robot Task compiler extension

#144 now makes standard RCC `robot.yaml` tasks a first-class capability kind. Extend this compiler rather than creating a permanent parallel Robot registry/compiler.

A current Robot/Task Package source snapshot should compile into the same Package Revision envelope with:

- package/source digest;
- Robot Task capability IDs/metadata;
- normalized `tasks` command/shell/robotTaskName declaration;
- `environmentConfigs` / legacy conda fallback normalized into the Environment Specification seam;
- PATH/PYTHONPATH/artifacts/preRun requirement metadata consumed by #143;
- `devTasks` recorded as development-only and excluded from ordinary production projection;
- no absolute imported robot path or `~/.robots` location in Package identity.

The compiler should validate metadata without running business tasks, then use the out-of-process environment/import boundary where execution is genuinely required.

#148 owns adversarial archive/URL ingestion before a remote Robot source becomes a trusted snapshot.

issues.acceptance_contract:
Parent: #133
Grounds the generic contract: #143
Related: #82, #90, #130, #144, `joshyorko/rcc#118`, `joshyorko/rcc#120`
Informs: #83, #129, #135, #139

## Outcome

Land the **first collapsed/local Actions consumer proof** of RCC Environment Artifacts and RCC-owned execution lifecycle before building the distributed worker fleet or freezing a supposedly generic adapter abstraction.

Replace the inherited model:

```text
RCC materializes once
 -> Actions caches PYTHON_EXE / CONDA_PREFIX
 -> Actions treats local paths as the environment contract
 -> Actions independently owns every later preparation decision
```

with:

```text
current package environment input
        |
provisional RccRuntimeAdapter seam
        |
RCC resolve/acquire/verify/materialize/lease
        |
fresh RCC-supported execution handle / env exec
        |
existing local Action import/process generation
        |
reap -> publish outputs -> RCC release
```

Preserve the excellent local developer experience. Do not require PostgreSQL, remote workers, Kubernetes, a remote provider, a new Package v3 authoring format, or a second runtime adapter for this first releasable slice.

## Proof-before-abstraction sequencing

This issue deliberately proves the concrete RCC path first.

- Implement the narrowest local adapter seam needed to consume RCC's supported contract.
- Keep adapter-specific details namespaced and explicit.
- Record every accidental Actions assumption discovered during integration.
- #143 then stabilizes the backend-neutral Runtime Adapter contract and proves it through a second implementation.
- Do not design a large plugin framework before the first real RCC Action process generation works end to end.
- Do not allow the first implementation to leak RCC-only fields into generic Run/Package identity merely because it landed first.

RCC is the first adapter, not the architecture.

## Why this remains a separate issue

#133 owns the complete RCC adapter/substrate relationship. #143 owns the long-term adapter-neutral contract. #90 owns independent workers.

This issue is the bounded bridge from today's current-v2 Action Server to the new RCC consumer contract:

- one Runtime process;
- current package directory authoring;
- current Action import/process pool;
- local SQLite/filesystem behavior;
- supported bundled/external RCC;
- real Environment Artifact identity and execution lease;
- no distributed topology prerequisite.

It must be independently reviewable, testable, releasable, and useful even if all later distributed work pauses.

## Historical and Control Room correction

Legacy RCC/Holotree already supported local reuse, shared mode, ZIP export/import, and `rccremote` delta pull. The new work must not claim Actions invented environment transfer.

The archived Control Room later added its own Holotree catalog service, cache, S3 sync, environment selector, RCC service, worker runner, and simulation paths. That product work is useful evidence of what operators need to see, but it also demonstrates what Actions should not duplicate now that RCC exposes Environment Artifacts, providers, leases, `env exec`, and `cache serve` directly.

This issue must not add a parallel:

- Holotree catalog database;
- object-index implementation;
- Environment Artifact provider server;
- provider-profile store;
- authoritative lease database;
- RCC simulation mode;
- local-path-based environment registry.

## RCC contract consumed

Target the stable supported surface delivered by `joshyorko/rcc#120`, using machine-readable equivalents of:

- Environment Specification resolution;
- Environment Artifact/Manifest identity;
- local acquire/build and verification;
- worker/process-owned lease;
- fresh execution handle or `rcc env exec`;
- release after process lifecycle completion;
- provider profile resolution where configured;
- lazy verified warm reuse without provider/network access;
- provider-backed acquire through `rcc cache serve` or another supported RCC provider;
- exact RCC feature/version compatibility evidence.

Existing `rcc run`, `rcc holotree variables`, v12 catalogs, bundles, export/import, and legacy `rccremote` may remain compatibility paths inside RCC. Actions should call the new supported consumer seam rather than reconstructing their internals.

Do not parse human CLI output or invoke hidden RCC commands.

## Provisional RccRuntimeAdapter seam

Provide the smallest typed local interface with semantics equivalent to:

- inspect/normalize current package environment input;
- resolve the RCC Environment Specification;
- acquire/build and verify the exact Environment Artifact;
- materialize and acquire a process-generation-owned lease;
- obtain a fresh execution handle or execute through supported RCC machinery;
- expose bounded cache/provider/provenance diagnostics;
- release only after every child is reaped and required outputs are published;
- fail clearly on missing RCC, missing feature, provider auth, incompatible platform, corrupt artifact, or preparation failure.

The adapter must not merely rename an activation environment dictionary.

The first process pool integration may still spawn/manage Python Action workers where that remains the supported Actions execution path. RCC environment identity, verification, lease, and handle derivation remain authoritative through the complete process generation.

## Current v2 package compatibility

Derive the first RCC Environment Specification from current spec-v2 `package.yaml` directories.

- validate and normalize environment declarations deterministically;
- keep mutable source generation separate from Environment identity;
- do not call the mutable directory a published Package Revision;
- keep the receipt migratable to #130/#135 immutable Package Revisions;
- apply request bindings/secrets only at execution, never to the Environment Artifact;
- preserve current source layout and authoring commands;
- support current Action import/discovery out of process in the exact leased environment.

## Autoreload and process generations

Preserve the workflow where a developer or model edits Actions and the running Runtime reloads without a manual restart.

### Source-only change

When the normalized RCC Environment Specification is unchanged:

1. reuse the exact verified Environment Artifact/materialization class;
2. retain/acquire the correct process-generation lease;
3. create and validate a new Action source/import process generation;
4. atomically route new catalog/calls to it;
5. let old calls drain or cancel under policy;
6. fully reap old children;
7. release only the old generation's lease/reference;
8. perform **zero** environment build, solver, provider, or cache-server calls.

### Environment-changing change

When dependency/environment input changes:

1. resolve a new Environment Specification;
2. acquire/build/verify/materialize it beside the old generation;
3. keep the old environment leased while old calls drain;
4. import/validate the new Actions out of process;
5. leave the old generation active on preparation/import failure;
6. atomically switch new calls/catalog to the ready generation;
7. release the old lease only after all associated processes are reaped and outputs complete.

An in-flight call never silently switches runtime or environment.

## Process-pool integration

Adapt the existing local Action process pool as the first execution-placement consumer.

Required behavior:

- process generation is keyed by source generation + selected runtime kind `rcc` + Environment Artifact identity + safe binding class;
- a fresh RCC-supported execution context is obtained at every process/process-generation boundary;
- warm process reuse cannot cross incompatible package/environment/binding/Workspace identity;
- the RCC lease lives for the complete child/process-generation lifetime;
- cancellation, terminate/kill escalation, and child reaping happen before release;
- stdout/stderr remain bounded/redacted;
- no reusable record contains request secret values;
- Runtime restart reacquires by RCC identity rather than trusting a stale executable path;
- verified local warm behavior continues when a configured provider is temporarily unavailable;
- RCC unavailability never falls back to simulation, raw Python, or another adapter.

## Migration from `env-info/<hash>.json`

Define a finite migration path:

- legacy cached activation files are not authoritative after the new adapter activates;
- an optional compatibility reader may inspect a legacy entry only to locate/revalidate state through RCC;
- every reused entry must be converted to current RCC identity/evidence;
- stale/missing executable paths trigger RCC reacquisition, not false readiness;
- no indefinite dual authority exists between `env-info` and RCC lifecycle state;
- cleanup and rollback behavior are explicit;
- old Runtime binary compatibility follows the declared release window.

## Runtime distribution and RCC lookup

Prove the same flow in:

- source development;
- wheel installation;
- frozen/PyInstaller Runtime binary;
- bundled/downloaded supported RCC;
- explicit external RCC override for development/testing;
- offline provider-free local mode;
- named provider profile with prepublished Artifact;
- Linux-first accepted matrix, followed by RCC #121 platform evidence.

RCC binary/version/feature negotiation must be machine-readable and fail closed.

## Provider-backed local proof

Although a provider is not required for normal local use, include one bounded proof that Actions consumes rather than reimplements RCC's remote capability:

1. publish an Environment Artifact through RCC;
2. serve it with `rcc cache serve` or use another supported RCC provider;
3. clear the second consumer's local materialization/content as appropriate;
4. acquire/verify/materialize through RCC;
5. run the same Action generation;
6. demonstrate a later verified warm execution with the provider unavailable and zero provider calls.

This is environment distribution, not remote Run execution.

## Observability

For each package/process generation expose compact evidence:

- selected runtime kind `rcc`;
- RCC and adapter contract version/features;
- Environment Specification/Artifact digest;
- provider profile/reference without URL credentials;
- local warm/provider/build acquisition class;
- resolve/build/acquire/verify/materialize/lease/activation timings;
- source generation/catalog revision;
- process-generation/lease identity as bounded local evidence;
- failure phase and actionable diagnostic;
- redaction/truncation indicators.

Do not make local paths high-cardinality primary metrics or portable IDs.

## Required tests

- deterministic source-versus-environment change classification;
- source-only reload with zero build/provider calls;
- generation activation/drain race;
- environment preparation/import failure rollback;
- process spawn/cancel/reap/lease ordering;
- Runtime restart and stale legacy cache;
- provider-dead verified warm path;
- `rcc cache serve` A→B consumer proof;
- corrupt/tampered/incompatible artifact rejection;
- secrets absent from environment/artifact/cache/argv/logs;
- missing/unsupported RCC classification with no fallback/simulation;
- current v2 package compatibility;
- source/wheel/frozen binary acceptance.

## Acceptance

1. Start the current local Runtime against a normal v2 package and resolve/acquire/materialize its real RCC Environment Artifact.
2. Execute an Action through the existing process pool while holding the RCC-owned lease/handle contract for the full process generation.
3. The Runtime no longer treats persisted `PYTHON_EXE`, `CONDA_PREFIX`, or a local Holotree path as authoritative identity.
4. Source-only reload changes the catalog/process generation with zero environment/provider work.
5. Dependency change creates a new environment beside the old while an in-flight call stays on the old generation.
6. Preparation or import failure leaves the old generation active.
7. Successful new generation activates atomically and releases the old lease only after drain/reap.
8. Restart with provider unavailable reacquires and executes from verified local RCC state.
9. Missing local materialization reacquires/rebuilds the same Environment identity instead of trusting stale paths.
10. One `rcc cache serve`/provider A→B proof passes without an Actions-owned cache server.
11. Missing/incompatible RCC fails explicitly with no simulation or adapter substitution.
12. Frozen binary runs the same lifecycle.
13. The implementation exposes a narrow seam that #143 can test and generalize without making generic schemas RCC-only.

## Non-goals

- Independent remote worker scheduling.
- Durable Run/Attempt database fencing.
- Full Package v3 projection/registry.
- Implementing uv/Python/Robot Framework adapters in this issue.
- A general plugin framework before the RCC proof.
- Kubernetes or a broker.
- RCC FUSE/zstd/packfile optimization.
- Removing safe current process-pool performance optimizations.
- Making every local Action call asynchronous.

Parent: #82
Generic adapter contract: #143
Depends on: #129, #130
Informs: #83, #86, #87, #90, #134, #138, #139, #142, #144
Cross-project implementation: `joshyorko/rcc#118`, `joshyorko/rcc#120`

## Outcome

Use RCC/Holotree as the **first full Capability Runtime Adapter** for Actions workers, consuming RCC's own supported environment identity, provider, materialization, lease, and execution lifecycle rather than treating RCC as a one-time bootstrap command or rebuilding RCC services inside Actions Runtime.

RCC is the default bundled adapter for compatible Action Packages and Robot Task Packages because it already provides:

- deterministic environment specifications and Holotree reuse;
- Environment Artifact manifests and cryptographic provider identities;
- local, offline, and warm-cache operation;
- environment publication/acquisition;
- worker-local materialization and process-owned leases;
- bounded execution through a fresh handle/`env exec` contract;
- Task Package/`robot.yaml` execution semantics;
- `rcc cache serve` and provider profiles for sharing immutable environment content.

This issue owns the **RCC-specific adapter integration**. #143 owns the backend-neutral Runtime Adapter contract. A future uv, raw Python, direct Robot Framework, or other adapter must be possible without pretending it has RCC-specific Environment Artifact fields.

## Architectural boundary

```text
Actions Runtime
  durable Package / Deployment / Run / Attempt / bindings / policy
                         |
                         v
              Capability Runtime Adapter (#143)
                         |
                         v
                    RCC adapter
       resolve -> acquire -> verify -> materialize
            -> lease -> execute -> release
                         |
                 local or provider-backed
              Holotree / Environment Artifacts
```

RCC owns environment reconstruction, content integrity, provider behavior, local materialization, execution leases, and RCC task launch semantics.

Actions Runtime owns:

- capability/package/deployment selection;
- authorization and binding references;
- durable Run/Attempt state and fencing;
- worker eligibility and scheduling;
- Work Item business lifecycle;
- output artifact metadata/authorization;
- UI/CLI/control-plane behavior.

RCC does **not** become Run authority, Work Item authority, the worker scheduler, or a required long-running daemon.

`rcc cache serve` is an Environment Artifact provider/cache service. Legacy `rccremote` is a compatibility cache-export protocol. Neither is a distributed Run coordinator or remote worker control plane.

## Design correction and archaeology

The inherited Action Server path uses RCC mainly as:

- `rcc ht hash`;
- `rcc holotree variables`;
- a source of cached `CONDA_PREFIX`, `PYTHON_EXE`, PATH, and environment dictionaries;
- a process-global helper guarded by local mutexes.

After bootstrap, Action Server owns the process lifecycle and treats local activation variables as the effective environment contract.

The archived `yorko-io/control-room` backend went in the opposite direction and duplicated too much above RCC:

- a large `RCCService` for robot detection, pulling, environment creation, execution, logging, and simulation;
- a separate `HolotreeService`, catalog API, cache, S3 synchronization, and environment UI;
- custom Robot distribution and worker agents;
- simulated success when RCC or remote deployment was unavailable;
- package/task/runtime state tied to local paths;
- Work Item and worker-capability inference based on names/source scanning.

Preserve Control Room's product intent—environments, packages, workers, runs, artifacts, diagnostics—but do not recreate its parallel RCC/Holotree implementation.

The new RCC branch already supplies the missing lower-level seam. Actions should consume it.

## RCC product surface to consume

Target the supported contract released by `joshyorko/rcc#118/#120`, including machine-readable equivalents of:

- Environment Specification resolution;
- Environment Manifest/Object Index identity;
- `rcc env publish`;
- `rcc env acquire`;
- `rcc env exec`;
- local content/materialization records;
- process-owned execution leases;
- named provider profiles;
- filesystem and HTTP provider behavior;
- lazy warm reuse with unavailable provider/credentials;
- `rcc cache serve`;
- existing `rcc run`, `rcc holotree variables`, v12 catalog, bundle, export/import, and `rccremote` compatibility where required during migration.

Do not bind Actions to CLI presentation text. Prefer supported JSON/machine contracts and explicit RCC feature/version negotiation.

Do not invoke hidden/removed RCC commands merely because a historical Action Server or Control Room branch used them.

## RCC adapter descriptor

The RCC implementation of #143 advertises at least:

- adapter kind `rcc`;
- RCC version/build and adapter contract version;
- supported RCC feature set;
- supported package/task formats and capability kinds;
- supported platforms/architectures/catalog readers;
- builder and Environment Artifact compatibility;
- local/offline/provider-backed preparation modes;
- provider profile capability and authentication requirements;
- Task Package semantics;
- cancellation/process-tree behavior;
- artifact/log/result behavior;
- supported execution placements;
- truthful limitations.

RCC availability is not a boolean inferred from `rcc version` alone. Preflight verifies every required feature for the selected package/plan.

## RCC-specific object model

These concepts remain first-class inside the RCC adapter and its typed receipts.

### Environment Specification

Canonical normalized dependency/build input derived from the Package Revision, including relevant platform, architecture, RCC/builder compatibility, and build policy.

### Environment Artifact

Verified platform-specific manifest/object closure produced or acquired through RCC. It is identified independently of provider URL, local cache path, or mutable tag.

### Local Materialization

Worker-local Holotree/runtime state derived from the exact artifact. It is cache/placement state, not durable Run identity.

### Environment Lease

Process-owned usage reference protecting the materialization/content while a process generation or Attempt uses it.

### RCC Execution Handle / receipt

Fresh bounded execution context produced under the lease. It may contain derived interpreter/environment/path values, but those are not portable identities.

Actions may store/reference:

- Environment Specification digest;
- Environment Artifact digest;
- platform/builder/feature compatibility;
- provider profile/reference under Deployment policy;
- RCC adapter/version evidence;
- materialization/cache class as bounded Attempt evidence.

Actions must not store as durable identity:

- `PYTHON_EXE`;
- `CONDA_PREFIX`;
- `ROBOCORP_HOME`;
- Holotree space/materialization paths;
- provider URLs;
- RCC process IDs.

## Required RCC adapter lifecycle

Implement #143 semantics through RCC-owned operations:

1. **inspect/resolve** package/task and normalized environment input;
2. **select/acquire** an exact compatible Environment Artifact or build locally under policy;
3. **verify/materialize** using RCC;
4. **lease** for the complete process/process-generation lifetime;
5. **execute** through a fresh RCC-supported handle/exec contract;
6. **stream/collect** bounded events, logs, result, and declared artifacts;
7. **cancel/reap** the full process tree under Attempt fencing;
8. **release** only after process reaping and output publication are complete;
9. **repair/evict** through RCC policy without invalidating durable identity.

Actions may wrap these operations with Run state/evidence. It must not implement another authoritative lease table, object index, Holotree catalog store, or provider-profile database.

## Local and provider-backed modes

### Zero-configuration local

```text
Package/capability
  -> RCC adapter
  -> local resolve/acquire/build
  -> local Holotree materialization + lease
  -> local process
```

No server, registry, login, container engine, or Kubernetes installation is required.

### Provider-backed worker

```text
Environment Artifact identity
          |
    RCC provider profile
          |
   rcc cache serve / HTTP provider / local mirror
          |
      worker-local RCC
 acquire -> verify -> materialize -> lease -> exec
```

Workers keep local caches. Provider/cache warmth is an optimization. The Run remains observable/controlable through another Actions Runtime replica because Run/Attempt authority is not in RCC or the provider.

Provider failure must still allow verified warm execution when RCC's contract permits it. Missing required content must fail as preparation unavailable, not trigger a different runtime adapter.

## Autoreload and process generations

Preserve current developer behavior.

### Source-only change

When the normalized RCC Environment Specification is unchanged:

- reuse the same verified Environment Artifact/materialization class;
- acquire/retain leases for old and new process generations correctly;
- import/validate a new Action source generation;
- atomically route new calls/catalog discovery to it;
- let old calls drain;
- release old generation lease only after complete process reaping;
- perform zero dependency resolution, environment build, or provider calls.

### Environment-changing change

- resolve a new RCC Environment Specification;
- acquire/build/verify/materialize beside the old generation;
- keep the old leased while in-flight work drains;
- validate the new process generation before activation;
- retain the old generation on failure;
- atomically activate success;
- release old resources after drain.

No in-flight call silently changes environment.

## Worker integration

#90/#138 workers advertise RCC adapter compatibility and cache locality as scheduling input:

- RCC/adapter contract version;
- platform/architecture/catalog readers;
- supported builder/artifact features;
- provider reachability where required;
- verified Artifact/materialization locality hints;
- preparation capacity/disk pressure;
- Task Package execution capability.

Registration is not authorization. A warm local path cannot substitute a different Environment Artifact or Package Revision.

The RCC adapter may run through local process, container, or independent-worker placement. Placement is orthogonal to the runtime adapter and is proven in #142.

## No lock-in rule

RCC-specific capabilities are represented as namespaced adapter features/receipts behind #143.

Generic Run/Package/Deployment schemas must not require:

- an RCC blueprint;
- a Holotree catalog;
- an RCC provider profile;
- a Holotree lease;
- an RCC binary.

A uv or direct Robot Framework adapter can satisfy the same generic lifecycle with its own typed runtime plan and weaker/stronger guarantees. There is no automatic fallback between them.

The generic interface is not considered stable merely because RCC works. At least one second adapter must pass the common conformance subset before the abstraction is declared backend-neutral.

## Trust and security

RCC/Holotree provides reproducibility and content integrity, not malicious-code sandboxing.

- never share writable live environments across mutually untrusted executions by default;
- verify artifacts/content before execution;
- prevent package-controlled cache/provider/config paths;
- keep secrets/bindings out of Environment Artifacts and reusable caches;
- use provider references/auth environment-variable names, never persisted credential values;
- register redaction before child output begins;
- keep worker/container/VM isolation in Worker Profile and execution placement policy;
- reject stale Attempt result/artifact publication independently of RCC process state.

## Performance and evidence

Measure phases separately:

- specification resolution;
- local build;
- provider publication/acquisition;
- verification;
- materialization/restore;
- lease and execution-handle creation;
- process startup;
- warm reuse;
- release/cleanup.

Expose compact evidence without making local paths primary metrics dimensions.

## Conformance and acceptance

Use #139 and the #143 adapter suite.

1. A current package resolves, acquires/materializes, leases, and executes through the RCC adapter locally.
2. Actions no longer treats cached `PYTHON_EXE`/`CONDA_PREFIX` as authoritative environment identity.
3. One environment is published/served through `rcc cache serve`, acquired by a clean worker, verified, materialized, leased, and executed without Actions implementing the provider/cache protocol.
4. Warm execution succeeds with provider unavailable when RCC's contract says all required verified content is local.
5. Source-only reload makes zero build/provider calls and preserves in-flight generations.
6. Environment-changing reload activates atomically and rolls back safely on build/import failure.
7. RCC feature/version/platform mismatch fails clearly; no simulation or backend fallback occurs.
8. Cancellation reaps the full process tree before lease release.
9. Secret values are absent from manifests, plans, command lines, cache metadata, logs, and receipts.
10. Local source, wheel, and frozen Runtime distributions consume the same supported RCC contract.
11. Generic Run/Package schemas remain valid for a conforming non-RCC adapter.

## Non-goals

- Making RCC a required daemon or the only runtime adapter.
- Reimplementing RCC Environment Artifact/provider/cache/lease internals in Actions.
- Replacing #83 Run/Attempt ownership or #90 worker scheduling with RCC locks.
- Requiring Kubernetes, containers, OCI, NFS, or a central build service.
- Treating RCC as a security sandbox.
- Completing every RCC storage/materialization optimization before the adapter contract is proven.

comments.body:
Cross-project implementation contract: https://github.com/joshyorko/rcc/issues/118

#118 now defines the concrete RCC/Holotree v1 path consumed here:

- cryptographic Environment Artifact Manifest + Object Index layered over existing v12 catalogs/current Hololib bytes;
- explicit separation between legacy SipHash cache keys and remote trust identities;
- provider publication/acquisition with verified objects and atomic manifest commit;
- worker-local materialization, lease, Execution Handle, and GC semantics;
- per-build/per-artifact/per-materialization coordination rather than a fleet-wide Holotree lock;
- source-only autoreload reuse and generation-safe environment-changing reload;
- a first two-process vertical that does not require a new catalog format, zstd, packfiles, FUSE, OCI, a daemon, or Kubernetes.

Treat #118 as the engine-side authority and this issue as the Runtime adapter/consumer contract.

## Implementation split and RCC lineage — 2026-08-18

This issue remains the full Actions environment-substrate contract. The board now has a bounded first implementation and explicit RCC dependencies.

### Immediate Actions child

- #134 — replace current local Action Server path/activation caching with an RCC EnvironmentBackend lifecycle while preserving current v2 package authoring, autoreload, frozen binary operation, and the existing process pool.

#134 is intentionally local/collapsed. It proves the Runtime can keep Environment Artifact/materialization/lease identity authoritative before #90 introduces independent workers.

### RCC program split

- `joshyorko/rcc#120` — release Environment Artifacts v1 / v18.19.0 handoff;
- `joshyorko/rcc#121` — supported-platform and ABI compatibility matrix;
- `joshyorko/rcc#122` — lease/crash/repair/GC semantics;
- `joshyorko/rcc#123` — production provider hardening;
- `joshyorko/rcc#124` — ZIP/bundle/offline/OCI carrier convergence;
- `joshyorko/rcc#125` — evidence-driven materializer/storage selection;
- `joshyorko/rcc#126` — provenance/SBOM/signatures/revocation/trust;
- `joshyorko/rcc#127` — cold-build coordination and prewarm.

Only #120 and the stable local lifecycle surface are prerequisites for the first #134 vertical. Do not block local Actions integration on every RCC follow-up.

### Historical correction

Legacy RCC already enabled same-platform portability through shared mode, Hololib ZIP export/import, and `rccremote` delta pull. The new contract does not invent transfer; it makes the existing capability cryptographically identified, natively publishable, usable from independent private RCC homes, lifecycle-managed through Materialization/Lease/Execution Handle, and schedulable by durable Artifact identity.

### Handoff invariant

Actions may retain Environment Specification/Artifact digests, compatibility policy, trust decision, and provider reference. It must not retain `PYTHON_EXE`, `CONDA_PREFIX`, `ROBOCORP_HOME`, Holotree paths, provider URL, or RCC PID as durable identity.

The old `env-info/<hash>.json` path must have one bounded migration/validation authority, not remain an equal second source of truth indefinitely.

issues.acceptance_contract:
Parent: #82
Depends on: #83, #90, #129
Informs: #85, #86, #92, #137, #141, #143, #144

## Outcome

Evolve `actions-work-items` from a strong producer-consumer library into a durable platform contract for queue-driven business work, retries, human review, schedules, and multi-step processing without conflating Work Items with capability Runs, worker Attempts, Runtime Adapter execution, Workflow state, or MCP Tasks.

Preserve current strengths:

- input reservation and parent-linked outputs;
- JSON payloads/files;
- business versus application failures;
- SQLite/File local mode and pluggable queue backends;
- Redis/DocumentDB experimentation;
- explicit queue names/deterministic local behavior.

Add distributed ownership/fencing/workflow semantics instead of timeout-only `IN_PROGRESS` reset.

## Terminology boundary

This issue uses **Work Item backend/queue adapter** for persistence/claim implementation.

Do not confuse it with **Capability Runtime Adapter** from #143 (`rcc`, `uv`, `python`, `robot-framework`).

A Work Item may initiate Runs executed through any compatible Runtime Adapter. Queue backend selection and runtime adapter selection are independent.

## Object boundaries

### Work Item

Durable unit of business work owning payload, attachments, queue identity, lineage, review, retry, scheduling, and human-visible lifecycle.

### Capability Run

One durable logical execution pinned to:

- Deployment Revision;
- Package Revision/capability;
- selected Runtime Plan/`runtime_kind`;
- binding/policy/Worker Profile;
- normalized input and optional Work Item link.

A Work Item may create zero/one/many Runs. A Run may have no Work Item.

### Attempt

One fenced worker try for a Run. Worker/adapter loss may create another Attempt under policy while preserving Run/Runtime Plan.

### Workflow Run / Node Run

Durable orchestration owned by #141. May coordinate Work Items and child capability Runs without replacing either.

### MCP Task

Protocol projection of long-running Run, not a queue item or business state.

Do not use one row/status enum as substitute for all concepts.

## Explicit Work Item capability mode

#143/#144 capability metadata declares whether Work Items are:

- `none`;
- optional input/output context;
- required single input Work Item;
- output creation allowed;
- queue claim/consumer;
- producer;
- workflow-managed.

Never infer Work Item behavior from:

- Robot/Task names such as Producer/Consumer;
- package/file names;
- source imports;
- presence of `actions-work-items`/Robocorp imports;
- selected Runtime Adapter;
- frontend defaults.

A standalone Robot Task/Action can run without Work Items when declared. An RCC task is not automatically a queue worker.

## Queue ownership model

Define backend-neutral claim semantics:

- `owner_id`/worker identity;
- DB/provider time for acquisition/expiry;
- heartbeat/renewable deadline;
- monotonic claim epoch/fencing token;
- conditional transitions requiring current owner/epoch;
- visibility timeout/redelivery policy;
- release/abandon/cancel/owner-loss reconciliation;
- idempotency/deduplication;
- partitioning/ordering/concurrency/fairness;
- not-before scheduling;
- explicit output publication authority.

Stale owner cannot complete/fail/mutate/create authoritative outputs after lease loss.

Queue backend protocol must expose conditional primitives; `release_input(item_id, state)` by ID alone is insufficient for distributed mode.

Work Item fence and Run Attempt fence are distinct and linked. Neither RCC lease nor runtime process ownership substitutes for either.

## Delivery semantics

State guarantees honestly:

- queue delivery and worker execution at least once unless backend proves stronger;
- external effects not exactly once by default;
- retries require stable capability/workflow idempotency key, effect receipt, transaction, or compensation;
- business failures normally do not auto-retry;
- application/infrastructure/adapter failures follow bounded policy;
- runtime-adapter outage does not cause adapter fallback;
- poison/exhausted work reaches dead-letter/review rather than infinite retry;
- changing Runtime Plan is restart/new Run/manual policy, not queue redelivery retry.

## Business lifecycle

Cover at least:

- pending/ready;
- claimed/executing;
- paused/blocked;
- needs review;
- completed;
- business failed;
- application/infrastructure failed/retryable;
- cancelled;
- dead-lettered/terminal error.

Separate queue ownership from business outcome. Lost claim is not business failure. A blocked child Run due to runtime unavailable/no worker is not automatically a failed business item.

## Retry/scheduling policy

- max attempts/age;
- fixed/exponential/provider backoff with jitter;
- error classification/override;
- not-before/scheduled creation;
- priority and queue concurrency;
- cancellation before/during execution;
- dead-letter/replay rules;
- retry provenance linking Work Item, Workflow, Run, Attempt, selected Runtime Plan, and effects;
- policy for long runtime preparation versus claim renewal/release;
- no capacity deadlock through held claims without progress policy.

## Human review/intervention

Durable APIs/audit for:

- `NEEDS_REVIEW` with reason/typed requested decision;
- approve/edit permitted fields/reject/cancel/retry/manual complete;
- evidence, files, notes;
- resume via new fenced claim;
- actor/time/auth/change provenance;
- exact Package/capability/Runtime Plan/protected input fingerprint;
- invalidation when protected runtime/effect context changes.

MRTR/MCP App/UI may deliver interaction, but review survives client loss and does not live in transport session.

Approving a Work Item does not authorize switching from RCC to uv or another Runtime Plan silently.

## Workflow and lineage

Preserve/define:

- parent-linked output Work Items;
- Work Item→Workflow/Node→Run→Attempt→adapter/worker→artifacts/effects→output Work Items;
- fan-out/fan-in;
- atomic visibility of outputs with input completion where supported;
- deterministic workflow recipes (#141);
- explicit payload/output schemas/versions;
- typed Work Item/data forwarding between nodes;
- standalone no-Work-Item capability path;
- parent/child Runs from #147.

A natural-language runbook/model transcript is not the durable workflow state machine.

## Queue backend model

### Local

SQLite/File remain simple development/single-node backends implementing public ownership semantics where applicable.

### Distributed

Production backend provides atomic claim, heartbeat, fencing, conditional transitions, and recovery. PostgreSQL, Redis, DocumentDB, SQS, or another backend may implement contract.

Do not reduce core semantics to weakest backend. Declare/test backend-specific guarantees/limits. Concurrent unique reservation alone is not production readiness.

Follow the same additive/conformance discipline as Runtime Adapters, while keeping the two adapter families typed separately.

## Relationship to Run/runtime ownership

Typical flow:

1. worker/workflow claims Work Item under Work Item epoch;
2. resolves capability/Deployment/Runtime Plan through shared admission;
3. creates pinned Run idempotently and records link;
4. one/more fenced Attempts execute through selected Runtime Adapter;
5. effect/result/artifacts are recorded;
6. only current Work Item owner/epoch commits authoritative business transition/outputs.

Define crash boundaries:

- before Run creation;
- after Run creation before link;
- during package/runtime preparation;
- after external effect before receipt;
- after result before Work Item completion;
- claim loss during review/wait;
- adapter/worker loss;
- cancellation races.

A replacement Run/Attempt preserves selected Runtime Plan unless policy explicitly creates a new Run. Queue redelivery never authorizes adapter substitution.

## Admission relationship

Work Item dispatch consumes #143 canonical AdmissionSnapshot:

- exact Work Item/payload version;
- capability/Runtime Plan/bindings/profile;
- off/shadow/enforce mode/generation;
- ready/blocked/unknown reasons.

Do not create queue-specific readiness heuristics.

In shadow, record evidence without changing dispatch. In enforce, only enrolled boundaries gate. Mode/generation captured per dispatch pass. Run creation revalidates protected identity.

## Required invariants

- one current fenced Work Item owner can transition authoritatively;
- stale owner cannot finish/fail/output;
- retry/redelivery visible;
- outputs/files linked to input and producing Workflow/Run/Attempt/Runtime Plan;
- Deployment/Package/capability/Runtime Plan pinned and retained through review/retry;
- Runtime Adapter selection never inferred from queue backend or changed on retry;
- human review survives restarts;
- provider/DB time, not worker clock, controls leases;
- queue and Run cancellation races defined;
- Work Item may exist without Run and Run without Work Item;
- no simulation/synthetic Work Item creation.

## Strict acceptance matrix

1. two workers race; one claim/epoch wins.
2. replacement claim rejects stale completion/output.
3. crash after external effect before completion avoids duplicate when receipt exists.
4. application failure retries; business failure does not by default.
5. exhausted item reaches dead-letter/review with provenance.
6. `NEEDS_REVIEW` survives restart and authorized decision.
7. scheduled item claimable no earlier than due time using DB/provider time.
8. one Work Item fans out to multiple pinned Runs/output items preserving lineage.
9. cancellation races with claim/runtime preparation/Run start/completion deterministically.
10. standalone Robot with `work_items=none` runs without queue/synthetic input.
11. optional Work Item Robot runs both with/without context.
12. selected RCC unavailable while uv installed: Work Item/Run does not fall back.
13. SQLite local and one service-backed backend pass same core suite.
14. queue backend and Runtime Adapter conformance/evidence remain separately typed.

## Non-goals

- Replacing capability Runs with Work Items.
- Treating MCP Tasks as queue.
- Exactly-once effects by assumption.
- Model/runbook as only workflow state.
- Requiring Kubernetes/SQS/Redis/DocumentDB.
- Reproducing every historical Control Room Work Item API/status.
- Inferring producer/consumer from Robot source/name.
- Selecting or falling back Runtime Adapters through queue policy.

Parent: #82
Follows cleanup: #125
Depends on: #129, #130
Informs: #71, #86, #87, #90, #126, #127

## Outcome

Define an open, inspectable provider contract for tabular data, federated queries, large result handles, ingestion, and knowledge retrieval without reviving Sema4.ai Data Server compatibility or making one data engine mandatory.

Preserve the useful product idea from Data Packages:

- packages declare logical data sources and named typed operations;
- deployments bind those declarations to environment-specific connections;
- Actions receive a managed typed client rather than raw credentials;
- large tabular results stay outside model context;
- long ingestion, indexing, and model-building work is durable.

Replace the closed implementation boundary:

- no downloaded `data-server-cli` requirement;
- no proprietary `sema4ai-data` dependency;
- no hidden `x-data-context` envelope;
- no assumption that each deployed agent needs its own opaque Data Server process;
- no required MindsDB, pgvector, cloud warehouse, or Kubernetes topology.

## Design archaeology

The former Sema4.ai design used Data Packages to declare external or generated Data Sources, `@query` and `@predict` functions, files, tables, models, and knowledge-base storage. Deployment resolved connection details and started a closed Data Server. Public artifacts also show a Sema4AI-integrated MindsDB image, but the exact closed Data Server internals and worker topology are not available for verification.

Useful references:

- https://sema4.ai/docs/solutions/data-access
- https://sema4.ai/docs/solutions/data-access/data-actions
- https://sema4.ai/docs/solutions/data-access/deploy
- https://sema4.ai/docs/solutions/knowledge-bases
- historical repository guide: `action_server/docs/guides/18-data-packages.md`

Treat these as design archaeology, not compatibility requirements.

## Required separation of concerns

### Package declaration

A Package Revision declares logical requirements and typed operations, for example:

- `data: customers` with expected capabilities such as SQL/read-only/schema discovery;
- `knowledge: policies` with search/filter/ingest capabilities;
- named query or operation definitions with typed parameters and output schemas;
- optional source bootstrap or migration declarations;
- effect, cost, row-limit, timeout, and authorization metadata.

It does not contain production credentials or provider-specific connection secrets.

### Deployment binding

Deployment #129 maps each logical requirement to a Workspace-scoped provider and connection reference. Changing a binding creates a new Deployment revision or otherwise produces an immutable Run-visible binding snapshot.

### Provider execution

Providers own connection establishment, query execution, result materialization, ingestion, indexing, and provider-specific lifecycle. A provider may be:

- direct PostgreSQL, MySQL, Snowflake, Oracle, or another database adapter;
- DuckDB or another local/offline engine;
- MindsDB for federated SQL and supported model/knowledge behavior;
- pgvector or another vector store;
- a remote service implementing the provider protocol;
- a future custom community implementation.

MindsDB is a useful reference provider, not the core architecture.

## Provider interfaces

The approved design may split tabular and knowledge contracts, but must cover these capabilities explicitly.

### Discovery and description

- provider and connection identity without exposing secrets;
- supported capabilities and version;
- catalogs, schemas, tables, columns, indexes, and selected statistics;
- knowledge collections, metadata fields, embedding/reranking configuration descriptors where applicable;
- cache revision, freshness, and authorization scope.

### Query and retrieval

- parameterized or prepared queries and named operations;
- safe native-query escape hatch under explicit policy;
- typed scalar/record results;
- large result materialization as durable handles rather than embedding all rows in MCP/model context;
- paging, slicing, filtering, aggregation, export, and expiration;
- semantic/vector search with metadata filters where supported;
- cancellation, timeout, row/byte/cost limits, and partial failure semantics.

### Mutation and lifecycle

- explicit read versus write capability;
- migrations/bootstrap with provenance and idempotency;
- file/table ingestion;
- knowledge indexing, re-indexing, and deletion;
- transaction or compensation semantics where the provider supports them;
- long operations represented as Action Runs and optionally Work Items, not synchronous protocol calls.

### Result handles

A large result or DataFrame reference must include durable identity, owner/Workspace scope, schema, provider provenance, creation and expiry metadata, access policy, and content/version fingerprint. It must be readable through another Runtime replica and exportable through the artifact plane where authorized.

MCP resources and resource templates should expose schemas, result metadata, pages, and artifacts when that is a better fit than a model-visible tool.

## Security and governance

- Credentials remain in secret or connection providers and are delivered only to the authorized execution boundary.
- Provider configuration is Workspace-scoped and access checked at both catalog and execution time.
- SSRF, private-network access, driver/plugin loading, file ingestion, archive extraction, and native queries have explicit policy boundaries.
- Queries and retrieval are parameterized by default; arbitrary SQL is not silently granted to every model or package.
- Read/write/consequential effects are represented in capability metadata and enforced.
- Audit records include Deployment, Package Revision, Run, provider, connection reference, named operation, actor, limits, result handle, and mutation outcome without logging credentials or sensitive rows.
- Bulk data does not enter model context unless explicitly requested and bounded.

## Local and distributed topology

The same contract must support:

- collapsed local mode using an in-process or child-process DuckDB/PostgreSQL/provider adapter;
- an independent provider service shared by authorized deployments;
- dedicated provider instances for strong isolation or special drivers;
- Kubernetes, VMs, or ordinary processes as implementation choices rather than package requirements.

Do not assume one Data Server per agent. Dedicated-per-deployment is an optional isolation profile with explicit cost and lifecycle, not the semantic model.

## Required invariants

- A Package Revision is portable across provider implementations that satisfy its declared capability contract.
- Equivalent Deployment bindings resolve deterministically and are visible in Run provenance.
- A binding change cannot silently change an in-flight Run's provider or credentials.
- Result handles are Workspace-scoped, authorization checked, and replica-safe.
- Provider failure cannot corrupt Run ownership or leave an unbounded synchronous MCP request hanging indefinitely.
- Retrying an externally mutating operation requires explicit idempotency or compensation semantics; exactly-once side effects are not assumed.
- Provider-specific extensions are namespaced and do not leak into the portable core contract by default.

## Smallest vertical

Prove the contract with two genuinely different providers rather than one abstraction over one implementation:

1. one local/offline provider, such as DuckDB over a packaged CSV or Parquet file;
2. one service-backed provider, such as PostgreSQL.

The vertical should include schema discovery, one parameterized named query, a large result handle, paging/export, authorization, cancellation/timeout, and Run provenance. A MindsDB or knowledge provider can follow once the core seam is real.

## Acceptance

- One Package Revision runs unchanged against two compatible provider bindings.
- Production connection secrets never appear in the package artifact, catalog schema, Run input/output, logs, or result-handle metadata.
- A large query returns a durable handle; rows can be paged and exported through another Runtime replica without entering model context by default.
- Binding updates create deterministic new deployment state while existing Runs remain pinned.
- A long ingestion or indexing operation survives client disconnect through Run/Task semantics.
- Read/write policy, parameterization, limits, cancellation, audit, and negative authorization are covered by tests.
- Local single-binary mode and split provider-service mode use the same public contract.

## Non-goals

- Reimplementing Sema4.ai Data Server byte-for-byte.
- Preserving `DataContext`, `x-data-context`, `DataServerTool`, or `sema4ai-data` compatibility.
- Making MindsDB, a vector database, or any cloud warehouse a mandatory dependency.
- Copying all enterprise data into Actions-owned storage by default.
- Treating RCC or a Python process as a security sandbox.
- Giving a model unrestricted SQL or network access merely because a provider exists.

Parent: #82
Depends on: #129
Informs: #71, #83, #89, #90, #120, #126, #127, #133, #135, #136, #139, #143, #144

## Outcome

Redefine an Actions Package from a mutable directory loaded into one Action Server into an **immutable portable capability bundle** that can be built once, deployed with environment-specific bindings, projected into bounded protocol surfaces, and executed through one of its explicitly compatible **Runtime Plans** on any eligible worker.

Preserve the strongest part of the original Robocorp/Sema4 design—typed Python plus hermetic RCC/Holotree environments—while making RCC the first rich runtime adapter rather than the package architecture itself.

A Package Revision describes:

- what capabilities exist;
- what source/resources implement them;
- what logical bindings they require;
- which runtime adapters/plans are explicitly compatible;
- what worker/placement capabilities they require;
- how Deployments may project them.

It does not own an Actions Runtime process, agent harness, Kubernetes object, production credential, worker-local environment path, provider URL, or arbitrary executable runtime plugin.

## Core separation

Keep these concepts distinct:

```text
Package Revision
  immutable source/resources
  capability graph
  runtime-plan declarations
        |
        +-- rcc plan: Environment Specification / Artifact requirements
        +-- uv plan: project/lock/Python requirements
        +-- python plan: interpreter/venv policy and weaker guarantees
        +-- robot-framework plan: suite/task + environment contract
        |
Deployment Revision
  selected/allowed Runtime Plan
  bindings/policy/projection/Worker Profile
        |
Run
  pinned Package + capability + selected Runtime Plan
        |
Attempt
  worker-local preparation/materialization/process/container evidence
```

Three axes must not be collapsed:

1. capability kind: Action, Robot Task, Workflow, Dev Task;
2. runtime adapter/plan: RCC, uv, Python, Robot Framework, future;
3. execution placement: local process, container, independent worker.

A Robot Task is not synonymous with RCC. A container image is not automatically an environment/runtime artifact. A Package Revision can be portable even when one runtime plan has platform-specific artifacts.

## Problem statement

The inherited model conflates:

- mutable authoring source;
- package/capability identity;
- dependency/environment resolution;
- one local RCC activation dictionary;
- import into one server process;
- broad Action/MCP catalog exposure;
- production context/bindings;
- local process-pool execution.

The first architecture pass risked replacing that with another lock-in: every Package/Run/worker schema required an RCC-shaped Environment Specification and Artifact even when a future uv, raw Python, or direct Robot Framework adapter would not have those concepts.

Package v3 must preserve RCC's richer identity where used without forcing fake RCC fields onto every backend.

## Required package contract

Exact syntax requires an approved specification before implementation.

### Identity

- logical Package ID/name;
- package schema version;
- semantic/version metadata where useful;
- immutable Package Revision digest;
- deterministic source/capability artifact digest;
- Runtime and `actions-core` compatibility range;
- compiler/normalization version;
- provenance/SBOM/signature metadata where available.

Package identity is independent of upload URL, local path, Runtime port, Deployment bindings, worker, provider, selected Runtime Plan, or mutable tag.

### Source artifact

Contains or references immutable implementation source/resources sufficient for an authorized worker to fetch, verify, inspect, import, and execute the selected capability.

Protect against traversal, escaping symlinks, archive bombs, mutable references, case/path ambiguity, special files, and mutation after snapshot.

Package source, runtime artifacts, and Run output artifacts are separately typed planes even when storage/CAS implementations are shared.

### Capability graph

Support typed capability identities for at least:

- Actions;
- Robot Tasks;
- Workflows/recipes;
- Dev Tasks as development-only;
- MCP resources/resource templates;
- prompts;
- App/Canvas resources and app-only dispatch.

Capabilities declare:

- package-scoped stable identity/schema;
- docs and typed inputs/outputs;
- effects/idempotency/side-effect policy;
- timeout/retry/cancellation/interaction metadata;
- Work Item support/requirement mode;
- expected artifacts/results;
- logical bindings and network/resource requirements;
- required worker/isolation capabilities;
- visibility/projection policy;
- compatible Runtime Plan IDs.

Capability identity is independent of model-visible MCP names and runtime adapter.

### Runtime Plan declarations

A Package Revision may contain one or more explicitly compatible, immutable normalized runtime-plan declarations.

Each plan includes:

- stable plan ID and `runtime_kind`;
- adapter contract version/range and required features;
- supported capability IDs/kinds;
- target platform/architecture/ABI constraints;
- adapter-specific normalized runtime specification/reference;
- source entrypoint/task declaration;
- preparation/build/acquisition requirements;
- runtime-specific artifact/provider references where portable;
- execution/config/artifact semantics;
- provenance/normalization receipt;
- truthful limitations.

Runtime-specific fields are namespaced and typed. Unknown required features fail closed.

A package may declare several plans only when the author/compiler can assert that each implements the same capability contract. The presence of `pyproject.toml`, `.robot`, or `robot.yaml` alone does not automatically prove cross-adapter equivalence.

### RCC Runtime Plan

RCC is the first/default plan for compatible Action and Task Packages and may declare/reference:

- supported RCC Task Package/action package semantics;
- canonical Environment Specification digest;
- platform/builder compatibility;
- optional/prepublished Environment Artifact references;
- required RCC feature/version;
- provider-profile capability requirements;
- task/entrypoint/PATH/PYTHONPATH/artifact/pre-run metadata.

RCC Environment Artifact/provider/materialization/lease behavior is owned by #133/#134 and `joshyorko/rcc#118/#120`.

The Package Revision never embeds as portable identity:

- `CONDA_PREFIX`;
- `PYTHON_EXE`;
- `ROBOCORP_HOME`/`RCC_HOME`;
- Holotree space/materialization path;
- provider URL;
- worker cache state.

### uv Runtime Plan

May declare/reference:

- normalized `pyproject.toml`/`uv.lock` or equivalent lock inputs;
- managed Python/version/platform requirements;
- explicit module/callable/script entrypoint;
- uv/adapter feature compatibility;
- cache/build/offline behavior and limitations;
- runtime identity/provenance distinct from local `.venv` paths.

Do not label a uv environment an RCC Environment Artifact.

### Python Runtime Plan

May declare:

- trusted system/managed/venv interpreter policy;
- exact Python compatibility;
- entrypoint/callable/script;
- dependency assumptions and reproducibility limitations;
- placement/trust restrictions.

A raw Python plan is deliberately weaker and policy-constrained rather than pretending to be hermetic.

### Robot Framework Runtime Plan

May declare:

- suite/task identity and source paths;
- runner/version/plugin requirements;
- explicit underlying Python/runtime preparation contract;
- Robot output/result artifacts;
- platform/placement limits.

Do not force a non-RCC Robot Framework package to manufacture fake `robot.yaml` or RCC identities.

### Adapter trust boundary

Packages declare compatibility with installed Runtime Adapter kinds. They do not ship executable Runtime Adapter plugins or choose arbitrary adapter binary paths, container sockets, provider URLs, image registries, cache roots, or privileged host capabilities.

Adapter installation, provenance, authorization, and feature negotiation are Runtime/operator concerns through #143.

### Logical bindings

Declare names and capability requirements without production values:

- secrets/OAuth;
- data/knowledge;
- Work Item queues;
- artifact/result stores;
- source/runtime-artifact providers;
- network/external-service grants;
- other provider capabilities.

Deployment #129 resolves declarations to Workspace-scoped references. Secret plaintext never enters Package or Runtime Plan artifacts.

### Exposure projections

A Deployment Revision selects a deterministic projection over the full internal capability graph.

Support at least:

- `direct`;
- `grouped` typed facades;
- `internal`;
- `app-only`;
- `resource` / `resource-template`;
- `prompt`;
- `workflow`.

Exposure is independent of runtime kind. A Robot Task may be internal, direct API/CLI, scheduled, workflow-only, or a bounded MCP facade without exposing arbitrary shell arguments.

Do not use one unrestricted `execute_anything` tool as the default catalog solution.

## Runtime selection and Deployment policy

The package declares compatible plans. Deployment #129 selects/defaults/constrains one or more allowed plans and Worker Profiles.

At Run creation:

- the selected capability and exact Runtime Plan are resolved;
- adapter compatibility/readiness is preflighted;
- selected `runtime_kind`, plan digest, adapter contract requirements, bindings, and Worker Profile are pinned;
- retries preserve that plan;
- no silent fallback occurs if the adapter becomes unavailable;
- a deliberate new Run may choose another declared/authorized plan.

A Package Revision does not promise feature parity across plans beyond its explicit declarations and conformance evidence.

## Source and runtime artifacts are separately portable

Conceptually:

```text
Package Revision source/capability artifact
        |
        +-- RCC Environment Specification
        |      -> platform Environment Artifact
        |      -> worker-local Holotree materialization
        |
        +-- uv project/lock specification
        |      -> adapter-specific prepared runtime/cache
        |
        +-- Python/Robot Framework runtime plan
               -> explicitly prepared local/worker state
```

Benefits:

- source-only changes can reuse compatible runtime artifacts;
- multiple packages may reuse immutable runtime content under policy;
- platform-specific artifacts are explicit;
- worker-local paths do not change Package identity;
- cold preparation can be published/shared when an adapter supports it;
- runtime-specific provenance remains inspectable without infecting generic identity.

Runtime reuse never authorizes sharing writable live state across mutually untrusted executions.

## Workflows versus model reasoning

Packages may declare durable deterministic workflows for ordering, retries, Work Item transitions, effects, fan-out/fan-in, and review.

External agent harnesses may still select high-level capabilities or reason inside designated Actions. Package v3 does not define a general agent reasoning harness.

Workflow nodes target capability IDs and pinned Runtime Plans through normal Run creation rather than embedding backend-specific shell commands.

## Package-owned state and migrations

- business/package state declarations remain separate from Runtime control-plane migrations;
- immutable revisions coexist while Runs drain;
- state compatibility/destructive migration boundaries are explicit;
- Deployment update cannot run arbitrary migrations silently;
- rollback accounts for data/state compatibility;
- runtime-adapter changes do not imply automatic business migration.

## Carrier and registry formats

Evaluate deterministic archives and OCI-compatible carriers while keeping identities independent of one registry/carrier/execution substrate.

Support:

- local authoring/import;
- air-gapped transfer;
- content-addressed storage;
- registry/provider distribution;
- multi-platform/runtime-plan metadata;
- verification without coordinator import/execution;
- separate Package source, runtime artifacts, and Run output planes;
- optional outer bundles that carry several planes without conflating their identities.

Do not force RCC Holotree contents into OCI when its catalog/content model is better, and do not force uv/Python plans into RCC manifests. OCI may be a carrier/index or placement artifact, not the universal runtime identity.

## Current-v2 and Robot compatibility

Provide explicit compiler/importer paths for:

- current spec-v2 `package.yaml` Action Packages;
- current `robot.yaml` Task Packages;
- existing generated metadata/cache files as evidence, not authority;
- future reviewed uv/Python/Robot Framework compatibility declarations.

Approximate flow:

```text
mutable source directory
      |
 safe immutable snapshot (#135/#148)
      |
 compile Package Revision + capabilities
      |
 installed adapters inspect/normalize compatible Runtime Plans
      |
 local Deployment Revision selects a plan
```

For the first Action v2 vertical, RCC may be the only declared plan. Do not silently reinterpret a mutable directory as a published revision or manufacture alternative adapter compatibility.

Compatibility does not preserve proprietary Data Server/context surfaces.

## Supply chain and trust

- cryptographic source/capability/runtime-plan identities;
- adapter-specific artifact digests where supported;
- SBOM/provenance/signatures;
- immutable publication after verification;
- dependency/lock source policy;
- out-of-process inspection/import;
- package-provided runtime plugin prohibition;
- runtime adapter and worker provenance;
- RCC/uv/etc. reproducibility does not equal malicious-code sandboxing;
- secret/network/worker grants remain explicit Deployment policy.

## Required invariants

- Package capability identity is independent of MCP name, Runtime process, runtime adapter, and placement.
- Package Revision identity is independent of worker-local runtime paths.
- Equivalent Package Revisions compile to equivalent capability/runtime-plan manifests.
- Equivalent Deployment projections compile to equivalent catalog revisions.
- Every Run pins one immutable Package Revision, capability, selected Runtime Plan, bindings, and Worker Profile.
- Retry never silently changes runtime adapter/plan.
- RCC-specific identities remain available when RCC is selected but are not mandatory generic fields.
- Production secrets are absent from Package and runtime artifacts.
- Worker cache warmth never substitutes a different package/runtime plan/artifact.
- Two packages may contain colliding internal names without global collision.
- local and remote workers consume the same normalized Package/Runtime Plan semantics.
- package source cannot install or authorize a Runtime Adapter.

## Conceptual layout

```text
my-package/
  package.yaml          # identity, capabilities, logical bindings, projections
  runtime/              # optional namespaced runtime-plan declarations
  src/
  resources/
  prompts/
  workflows/
  migrations/
  tests/
```

This layout is illustrative, not an accepted syntax decision. Existing RCC `robot.yaml`/environment files remain valid compatibility inputs where supported.

## Acceptance

1. One source tree builds byte-identical immutable Package Revision/capability/runtime-plan artifacts twice.
2. A current package compiles one valid RCC Runtime Plan and executes through #133/#134 without local paths in identity.
3. A representative Robot Task package exposes multiple Robot Task capabilities and RCC plan metadata.
4. One uv or direct Robot Framework fixture compiles a non-RCC Runtime Plan and executes through the same generic Run model without fake RCC fields.
5. A package declaring RCC and uv compatibility never falls back automatically; each deliberate Run pins one plan.
6. The same Package Revision runs locally and on an independent worker without source mutation.
7. Worker B acquires a prebuilt RCC Environment Artifact through RCC when the RCC plan is selected.
8. A broad connector exposes a bounded model catalog while retaining typed internal capabilities.
9. Direct/grouped/internal/app/resource/prompt/workflow projections are deterministic.
10. Deployment update/rollback does not mutate revisions or redirect in-flight Runs.
11. Package verification emits source/capability/runtime-plan provenance without importing untrusted code into the coordinator.
12. Current v2/Robot packages have explicit compatibility fixtures.
13. Package-provided runtime plugin/binary/provider/socket selection is rejected.

## Non-goals

- Building an agent reasoning harness.
- Requiring every package to support multiple runtime adapters.
- Replacing MCP.
- Treating a package as a Kubernetes manifest.
- Requiring Kubernetes, OCI, or an RCC daemon.
- Hiding all capabilities behind an untyped router.
- Treating local runtime paths as Package identity.
- Making Canvas own an incompatible package/runtime revision system.
- Claiming identical semantics across adapters without conformance evidence.

Parent: #82
Informs: #83, #85, #87, #89, #90, #91, #95, #130, #133, #138, #143, #144

## Outcome

Define the **capability deployment object model** between immutable Package Revisions/runtime-plan declarations and a running Actions Runtime installation.

The Runtime must answer durably and deterministically:

- which immutable Package Revision is deployed;
- which capabilities are exposed;
- which Runtime Plans/adapters are allowed or selected for each capability;
- which adapter-specific runtime artifact/specification policy applies;
- which secret, OAuth, data, artifact, Work Item, source/runtime provider, network, and worker bindings apply;
- which Worker Profile/placement requirements apply;
- which exact snapshot an existing Run/retry remains pinned to during update/rollback;
- whether execution may be local, container-backed, or independent-worker.

Do not encode these answers implicitly in startup flags, mutable directories, inherited host environment, local Holotree/venv paths, package-selected executable plugins, Kubernetes objects, or the Runtime replica that received the request.

## Scope boundary

A Deployment here is an **Actions capability deployment**, not an agent deployment and not a historical Control Room application object.

An external agent runtime may attach through MCP/HTTP. Actions Runtime does not own its conversation, planning, model settings, memory, or runbook lifecycle.

A Workspace is a semantic trust/policy/provider/audit namespace. It does not require a Kubernetes namespace or highly multi-tenant SaaS topology. Single-Workspace-per-installation remains a safe first-class profile.

## Design archaeology and correction

Historical Sema4/Control Room designs correctly separated authored package content from production secrets/connections and provided Workspace-scoped worker/runtime selection. They also often coupled process/task execution to proprietary compute products, one specific RCC/Holotree environment model, local paths, or Kubernetes runtime objects.

The useful lessons are:

- keep production bindings separate from Package source;
- version Deployment state atomically;
- support draft/test→production binding/policy changes;
- make runtime/worker capability requirements explicit;
- keep in-flight Runs pinned;
- keep physical topology simpler than the semantic graph.

The first Actions architecture revision still assumed every Deployment selected an RCC-shaped Environment Artifact. This issue now selects a generic Runtime Plan from #130/#143 while preserving RCC-specific identities inside the RCC plan.

## Required object model

Exact table/API names require approved design.

### Workspace

Trust, policy, identity, quota, provider registration, connection reference, adapter authorization, worker trust, and audit namespace.

Do not derive it from filesystem path, port, process, Kubernetes namespace, agent ID, or provider account name.

### Package

Logical reusable package identity.

### Package Revision

Immutable source/capability/runtime-plan manifest from #130/#135.

It declares capabilities and one or more explicitly compatible Runtime Plans. It does not contain production bindings or worker-local runtime state.

### Capability

Package-scoped Action, Robot Task, Workflow, Dev Task, resource, prompt, or App capability identity.

Deployment decides visibility, bindings, runtime selection, Worker Profile, and policy independently from the capability's source identity.

### Runtime Plan

Immutable normalized package declaration from #130/#143 describing how a capability can be prepared/executed by one Runtime Adapter.

Common identity includes:

- `runtime_kind`;
- plan digest/schema;
- adapter contract/version/required features;
- platform/architecture/ABI requirements;
- adapter-specific runtime specification/reference;
- supported capability IDs;
- provenance and limitations.

Examples:

- RCC plan with Environment Specification/Artifact policy and provider-profile requirements;
- uv plan with normalized project/lock/Python requirements;
- raw Python plan with explicit interpreter/trust restrictions;
- direct Robot Framework plan with suite/task and environment contract.

Runtime-specific fields remain namespaced. A Deployment never fabricates an adapter plan that the Package Revision did not declare.

### Runtime Policy / selection

A Deployment Revision may:

- select one default Runtime Plan for a capability;
- allow a bounded set of declared plans;
- lock runtime selection entirely;
- allow authorized caller selection among declared plans;
- constrain adapter versions/features/provider profiles;
- prohibit weaker adapters/placements in production;
- require conformance/trust/signature evidence.

There is no implicit fallback order.

If a Deployment selects RCC and RCC is unavailable, the Run is blocked/failed according to policy. It does not silently become uv/Python/Robot Framework. A deliberate new Run may select another allowed plan explicitly.

### Deployment

Stable Workspace-scoped logical identity.

### Deployment Revision

Immutable snapshot selecting:

- Package Revision(s);
- capability projection;
- allowed/default Runtime Plan(s);
- adapter-specific runtime artifact/provider policy;
- provider bindings;
- authorization/policy revision;
- Worker Profile(s)/placement constraints;
- concurrency/quotas/runtime limits;
- compatibility/migration metadata;
- audit/provenance references.

Updating a Deployment publishes a new immutable revision and atomically advances the pointer for **new** Runs. Old revisions remain addressable while pinned Runs drain or are retained.

### Binding

Map logical requirements to Workspace-scoped provider references, for example:

- `secret: salesforce_client_secret`;
- `oauth: google_user`;
- `data: customers`;
- `knowledge: policies`;
- `queue: remittances`;
- `artifacts: reports`;
- `package-source: package-registry`;
- `runtime-artifact: rcc-office-cache`;
- `network: vendor_api`.

Package/Runtime Plans contain declarations and provider capability requirements, never production secret values or arbitrary provider URLs.

### Capability Projection

Deterministic MCP, HTTP, event, workflow, CLI, and App surface selected from the full Package graph.

Projection is independent of Runtime Plan. The same Robot Task may be internal-only, scheduled, CLI/API, workflow-node, or a bounded MCP facade while executing through RCC or another declared adapter.

#130 owns projection semantics; #89 owns deterministic catalog revisions.

### Worker Profile

Execution/placement requirements used by #90/#138, including:

- allowed runtime kinds/adapter contracts/features;
- platform/architecture/ABI;
- resource class/capacity;
- network/provider grants;
- browser/desktop/GPU/device capabilities;
- container/local/VM placement support;
- trust domain/Workspace scope;
- isolation/dedication policy;
- cache/locality hints as optimization.

A Worker Profile is not a PodSpec and cannot substitute an undeclared Runtime Plan.

## Draft/test versus production mapping

Support:

```text
same Package Revision
       |
 test Deployment Revision
   runtime: python-local or rcc-test
   data:test-db
   oauth:test-user
       |
 promote / publish
       |
 production Deployment Revision
   runtime: rcc-prod or uv-prod (only if declared)
   data:prod-db
   oauth:prod-service
   worker: private-vpc
```

Promotion may change bindings, Runtime Plan selection, Worker Profile, limits, policy, and projection while reusing exact source. Each state is immutable/provenanced.

Do not mutate one revision from test to production or assume a test-compatible runtime is production-approved.

## Resolution and Run pinning

Before a Run becomes executable, resolve a durable snapshot containing at least:

- Workspace;
- Deployment + Deployment Revision;
- Package Revision;
- capability identity/schema;
- selected Runtime Plan + `runtime_kind` + plan digest;
- required adapter contract/version/features;
- adapter-specific runtime specification/artifact/provider policy;
- binding references/policy versions;
- Worker Profile/placement constraints;
- catalog revision where visible;
- actor/authorization decision reference;
- idempotency/effect scope.

#83 owns Run/Attempt state and records this snapshot.

A replacement Attempt for one Run uses the same snapshot. It does not:

- resolve the current Deployment;
- select a different adapter;
- adopt a newer environment/lock/interpreter;
- change bindings/worker policy;
- reinterpret a mutable tag.

A deliberate **restart/new Run** may resolve current Deployment and choose another allowed plan.

## Runtime/provider availability

Preflight distinguishes:

- Runtime Adapter installed/available;
- authentication/provider binding required;
- runtime preparation/build/acquisition needed;
- compatible runtime content already warm;
- incompatible adapter feature/platform;
- no eligible worker/placement;
- forbidden selection.

RCC provider profiles/`cache serve` are environment-content providers, not Deployment or Run authority. Deployment stores authorized references/policy, not RCC object indexes or provider credentials.

Equivalent concepts for uv/other adapters remain namespaced.

## Physical topology doctrine

### Zero-config local

```text
one actions-runtime process
  synthesized/default Workspace
  SQLite
  local Package generations
  bundled RCC adapter by default
  optional installed local adapters
  local filesystem providers
  local worker/process pool
```

No remote provider, container engine, Worker registration, or Kubernetes requirement for ordinary local execution.

### Simple production

```text
1..N Runtime replicas
     |
 shared PostgreSQL
     |
1..N independent workers
  each advertising Runtime Adapter + placement capabilities
     |
shared immutable/provider backends as configured
```

### Strong isolation

Separate installations, Worker Profiles, pools, or trust domains may isolate Workspaces, browsers, desktop sessions, consequential work, or untrusted packages.

Semantic richness must not force physical microservice sprawl.

## Update, rollback, drain, and retention

Define:

- atomic current-revision updates;
- optimistic update conflicts;
- rollout validation of Package, Runtime Plan, adapter, provider, and Worker Profile compatibility;
- N+1 for new Runs while N Runs/retries remain pinned;
- old adapter/runtime artifact/worker support while N drains;
- explicit blocked/manual-recovery outcome if old plan can no longer be satisfied;
- rollback changing new resolution only;
- retention/GC references for Package Revisions, Runtime Plans/artifacts, Runs, Tasks, Work Items, artifacts, catalogs, and audit;
- no historical provenance rewrite.

Revoking an adapter/runtime artifact/provider may block new Runs and has explicit behavior for active Runs; it does not mutate old records.

## Authorization and isolation

- Workspace authorization covers Deployments, Packages, Runtime Plans, adapters, bindings, catalogs, Runs, artifacts, Tasks, Work Items, and provider resolution.
- Knowing a plan/digest/provider reference does not grant access.
- Package code cannot choose an unauthorized adapter, provider, Worker Profile, image, socket, or mount.
- Secret plaintext never enters Package/Deployment/Run/catalog/log metadata.
- Worker eligibility includes Workspace/trust authorization and Runtime Plan compatibility.
- multi-Workspace mode requires negative tests; single-Workspace isolation remains supported.
- host/Kubernetes isolation strengthens but does not replace application authorization.

## Required invariants

- resolving a request yields one immutable Deployment Revision, capability, Runtime Plan, and catalog revision.
- every Run pins Deployment, Package, capability, Runtime Plan, bindings, and Worker Profile before execution.
- retry never switches adapter/plan.
- RCC-specific Environment identities are retained when RCC is selected but absent as mandatory generic fields.
- update/rollback never redirects an in-flight Run.
- old/new revisions coexist while Runs drain.
- production bindings/secrets are separate and authorized.
- worker/cache locality is optimization, not identity or authorization.
- local and distributed modes preserve object semantics.
- mixed Runtime/worker/adapter versions fail closed when required correctness cannot be enforced.

## Deliverables

- ADR for Workspace/Package/Capability/Runtime Plan/Deployment/Worker relationships;
- database schemas/migrations and typed APIs;
- endpoint→Deployment→capability→Runtime Plan→pinned Run resolution flow;
- runtime selection/no-fallback rules;
- promotion/update/rollback/drain/retention/GC rules;
- authorization/audit boundaries;
- local-default Workspace behavior;
- compatibility path for mutable current packages/Robot imports;
- negative scenarios for package-provided adapter authority.

## Acceptance

1. Two Runtime replicas resolve the same Deployment Revision to identical Package, capability, Runtime Plan, bindings, policy, Worker Profile, and catalog identities.
2. A rolling update starts new Runs on N+1 while N Runs/retries preserve exact N runtime plan.
3. Rollback affects new Run resolution only.
4. Test/production Deployments reuse one Package Revision with different authorized bindings/runtime policies.
5. A package declaring RCC and uv can be deployed with one explicit default; missing default adapter never triggers fallback.
6. A worker lacking selected adapter/features/platform/provider access declines safely.
7. RCC Deployment can reference an authorized provider profile/Environment Artifact policy without copying provider URL/credentials or object protocol into Deployment state.
8. A non-RCC Deployment pins its own plan without fake RCC fields.
9. Local single-binary mode proves the same lifecycle without external infrastructure.
10. single-Workspace and multi-Workspace profiles reject cross-boundary references correctly.
11. restart-as-new may select current plan while ordinary retry remains pinned.
12. mixed-version rollout fails safely.

## Non-goals

- Building an agent harness.
- Building a Kubernetes operator.
- Requiring one pod/server/namespace per package.
- Requiring multi-tenancy.
- Recreating Control Room compatibility or proprietary compute products.
- Choosing one secret, queue, artifact, data, source, runtime-artifact, or environment provider.
- Implementing Runtime Adapter internals or automatic fallback here.

comments.body:
MCP Apps template ownership clarification (architecture triage; no implementation authorization)

This issue owns the turnkey community MCP Apps/Canvas template and its end-to-end conformance evidence:
package.yaml -> actions.py -> tool _meta.ui.resourceUri -> registered ui:// resource -> text/html;profile=mcp-app -> resources/read -> standard sandboxed host bridge and tool round trip.

Dependencies:
- #99: MCP App View resource/bridge/renderer and host-side conformance;
- #100: smallest supported public package-author API in actions-core/actions.mcp;
- #125: community cleanup;
- accepted post-cleanup frontend base.

Do not invent a separate Canvas protocol, Python Canvas runtime, or standalone MCP client. This is not an immediately writable post-#125 lane; implementation waits for #99/#100 and the accepted frontend base.

Official extension evidence: https://github.com/modelcontextprotocol/ext-apps/blob/10195ad91851502134930e9b80ec2c04e277a720/specification/2026-01-26/apps.mdx

## Template target after #129/#130

When this template is designed, it should demonstrate the new portable contract rather than package another mutable directory plus server assumption:

1. build one deterministic immutable Package Revision;
2. declare logical secret/data/artifact/queue requirements without production values;
3. create a local Deployment Revision that resolves those bindings;
4. compile a bounded MCP/App capability projection;
5. execute a pinned Run and expose Task/artifact/result handles where needed;
6. update and roll back without mutating either published package revision.

The template may collapse registry, resolver, coordinator, worker, SQLite, filesystem storage, and local providers into one binary for the user experience. Conceptually it must use the same identities and lifecycle as split mode.

Do not reintroduce Data Server compatibility, `x-data-context`, one Action Server per app, private services, or an unbounded catalog of every internal generated operation.

## Architecture archaeology decision

Keep #125 and PR #128 as a clean removal of the closed product boundary.

The useful Data Package ideas are being carried forward separately in #131 as an open provider contract. That follow-up must **not** block this cleanup and must not preserve or reintroduce:

