issues.acceptance_contract:
Parent: #82
Depends on: #83, #84, #129, #130, #143
Related: #86, #87, #131, #132, #133, #138, #142

## Outcome

Remove the assumption that the Runtime process receiving an API, MCP, event, schedule, CLI, or App request must also own the process/container that executes the capability.

Runtime A may resolve a Deployment and create a Run, Worker C may execute a fenced Attempt through the selected Runtime Adapter and placement backend, Runtime B may serve status/cancellation, and providers may own package source, runtime artifacts, outputs, secrets, data, and Work Items.

Keep the current local process pool as the collapsed single-node worker/placement implementation. Do not delete the simple path, require Kubernetes/broker, or turn every semantic role into a service.

## Scope boundary

This is a **capability execution worker and placement plane**, not an agent harness.

A worker executes one pinned capability/Runtime Plan under one fenced Attempt. It does not own model planning, conversation memory, runbooks, LLM routing, Deployment resolution, or general agent lifecycle.

## Three-axis correction

Worker design must distinguish:

1. **Capability kind** — Action, Robot Task, Workflow node, etc.;
2. **Runtime Adapter** — RCC, uv, Python, Robot Framework, future;
3. **Execution placement/backend** — local process, container, VM/independent worker, future sandbox.

A worker may support multiple adapters and placements. A container is not a runtime adapter. RCC's cache/provider server is not a worker scheduler. A Robot Task is not synonymous with RCC.

## Ownership boundaries

### #83 — Run/Attempt authority

Owns durable state machines, claim/lease/epoch fencing, cancellation intent, immutable pinning, reconciliation, and authoritative terminal/result transitions.

### #129/#130/#143 — resolution and Runtime Plan authority

Own:

- Package/capability identity;
- declared compatible Runtime Plans;
- Deployment selection/policy;
- adapter contract/features;
- namespaced runtime specification/artifact requirements;
- binding and Worker Profile snapshot;
- preflight/no-fallback behavior.

### Runtime Adapter implementations

- #133/#134 own RCC-specific Environment Artifact/provider/materialization/lease/exec semantics;
- #143 owns the generic adapter contract;
- future uv/Python/Robot Framework adapters own their own preparation/launch details.

### This issue — worker and placement authority

Owns:

- worker registration/readiness/drain;
- scheduling eligibility/capacity;
- adapter/placement capability advertisement;
- package/runtime locality hints;
- claim execution after #83 ownership;
- package acquisition orchestration;
- invoking selected adapter prepare/start/events/cancel/collect/release;
- process/container spawn and supervision;
- log/event backpressure;
- cancellation delivery/escalation;
- child reaping/orphan cleanup;
- resource/placement policy;
- shutdown/recovery.

Every authoritative operation carries current Attempt ID/owner/epoch.

No worker, adapter, container engine, cache service, or broker creates a second Run authority.

## Required worker model

Worker registration/heartbeat exposes policy-checkable information including:

- stable worker ID and ephemeral instance ID;
- worker protocol versions/features;
- Runtime/package/capability schema compatibility;
- installed authorized Runtime Adapter descriptors:
  - `runtime_kind`;
  - adapter contract/implementation version/provenance;
  - supported package/capability formats;
  - required/optional features;
  - platform/architecture/ABI;
  - preparation modes/providers;
  - cancellation/result/artifact guarantees;
- supported placement backends:
  - local process;
  - container engine/profile;
  - native desktop/session;
  - future sandbox/VM;
- resource capacity/current availability;
- accepted Worker Profiles/trust domains/Workspace scopes;
- network/secret/data/artifact/browser/desktop/GPU/device capability classes;
- Package/source cache locality hints;
- adapter-specific runtime cache hints:
  - RCC Environment Artifact/materialization digests;
  - uv/runtime caches where safely describable;
- preparation/build/hydration capacity and disk pressure;
- readiness/drain/degraded/auth-required state;
- software/build provenance and compatibility window;
- provider-time heartbeat evidence.

Registration advertises capability; it does not grant authorization, ownership, package trust, provider access, or permission to substitute a Runtime Plan.

## Scheduling input

Consume immutable identities resolved before execution:

- Workspace/Deployment Revision;
- Package Revision/capability;
- selected Runtime Plan ID/digest and `runtime_kind`;
- adapter contract/version/required features;
- adapter-specific runtime specification/artifact/provider policy;
- Worker Profile/placement/resource requirements;
- binding references, never plaintext values;
- Run/Attempt ID/epoch;
- input/artifact/optional Work Item references;
- idempotency/effect/retry/priority/timeout/cancellation policy.

Do not schedule by mutable directory, function object, task name alone, local process slot, interpreter/Holotree/venv path, container ID, or whichever Runtime imported source.

Do not schedule a pinned RCC plan onto a uv-only worker because uv could theoretically run Python. Compatibility must be explicitly declared and a different plan requires a different Run.

## Worker execution lifecycle

After #83 grants a fenced Attempt:

1. validate current Attempt ownership/epoch and cancellation intent;
2. fetch/verify exact Package Revision through #136;
3. validate worker still supports selected adapter/placement/policy;
4. invoke the selected Runtime Adapter to prepare the exact pinned plan:
   - RCC acquire/verify/materialize/lease;
   - uv prepare locked project/Python;
   - Python validate explicit interpreter/runtime policy;
   - Robot Framework prepare runner/environment;
5. resolve authorized provider grants/bindings for this Attempt;
6. record package/runtime/worker/placement preparation evidence durably;
7. transition conditionally to spawning/running;
8. start via the authorized placement backend under a managed process/container/session identity;
9. stream bounded ordered events/logs and provisional artifacts under current fence;
10. observe heartbeats/cancellation/ownership;
11. collect adapter result/effect/output receipts;
12. conditionally publish/finalize outputs and Attempt outcome through #83/#86;
13. fully reap/remove child process/container/session;
14. release adapter/runtime/package resources after output lifecycle completes.

A warm cache may skip expensive work but never verification, pinning, lease/resource ownership, or fencing semantics.

## No fallback, simulation, or heuristic capability inference

Follow #143/Hive doctrine:

- runtime kind is explicit;
- missing adapter, auth-required, provider unavailable, incompatible feature, no capacity, and execution failure are distinct;
- selected adapter never silently falls back;
- worker does not simulate success when RCC/uv/Robot Framework/container infrastructure is unavailable;
- health/version success is not real execution evidence;
- worker capabilities come from signed/configured descriptors and tested profiles, not names such as `website`, `email`, `document`, or task source scans;
- Work Item modes come from declarations, not `Producer`/`Consumer` naming.

A worker may decline an Attempt safely. Decline does not mutate the Run into a different Runtime Plan.

## RCC integration doctrine

RCC is the first/default rich adapter.

Use #133/#134 and `joshyorko/rcc#118/#120` deeply:

- Environment Specification/Artifact identity;
- publish/acquire/verify/materialize;
- process-owned leases;
- `rcc env exec`/supported execution handle;
- provider profiles;
- lazy verified warm reuse;
- `rcc cache serve` environment distribution;
- local/offline operation;
- Task Package semantics.

Workers prefer local RCC/Holotree caches. Do not require shared writable NFS/Hololib or use RCC local locks as fleet ownership.

`rcc cache serve`/legacy `rccremote` distribute environment/cache content. They do not register workers, claim Attempts, carry cancellation authority, or own Run state.

Actions workers must not implement another RCC object store/catalog/cache server/provider-profile/lease authority.

## Other adapter doctrine

A uv/Python/Robot Framework adapter advertises its own typed capabilities and limitations. Generic worker protocol must not require RCC manifests/catalogs.

Examples:

- uv worker can prepare exact normalized lock/Python plan;
- raw Python worker may be restricted to trusted/local profiles and explicit interpreter contract;
- Robot Framework adapter can execute declared suites/tasks and collect native output files;
- unsupported RCC-only features cause safe decline, not emulation.

Adding an adapter is an independent #139 conformance slice.

## PlacementBackend contract

The existing local process pool becomes one placement implementation rather than semantic owner.

A placement backend covers:

- capacity/queue depth;
- process/container/session creation;
- working/source/runtime mount/view;
- resource limits;
- network/mount/device policy;
- stdout/stderr/event collection with backpressure;
- cancellation/terminate/kill escalation;
- process groups/child reaping/orphan detection;
- restart/drain;
- execution receipt;
- no unmanaged PID/container/cloud ID as client identity.

Future placements may include rootless container, VM, Windows desktop/session, browser worker, Dagger, WASM, or sandbox. They implement the same bounded fenced Attempt contract where applicable.

Runtime Adapter and placement are composed deliberately. Not every adapter supports every placement.

## Container placement security

The controlling worker may hold container-runtime authority under operator policy. Package code does not receive the socket/API.

Default deny:

- privileged mode;
- host PID/network namespace;
- arbitrary root/socket/credential/cache mounts;
- unbounded devices/capabilities;
- package-selected images/registries;
- Docker-in-Docker by default;
- package-provided adapter binary;
- undeclared network access.

Images are selected/pinned by Worker Profile/operator policy and remain distinct from Package/runtime artifact identity.

## Provider access

Workers resolve authorized handles for:

- secrets/OAuth (#87);
- outputs/artifacts (#86);
- data/knowledge (#131);
- Work Items (#132);
- Package source (#136);
- adapter-specific runtime artifacts/providers (#133/#143).

Credentials are narrow/short-lived where possible, delivered only to the execution boundary, registered for redaction before output, and absent from reusable process/cache/package/runtime metadata.

Warm process reuse cannot leak request/Workspace/provider state.

## Claim/process protocol

Define:

- atomic claim/owner/DB-time lease/epoch (#83);
- heartbeat/renewal;
- conditional preparation/start/spawn/finish/fail/cancel acknowledgements;
- crash-before/after preparation/spawn reconciliation;
- durable adapter/placement execution receipt;
- cancellation delivery/escalation/ack;
- stale-owner rejection for events/results/artifacts/effects/Work Items;
- safe decline for adapter/placement/policy/capacity mismatch;
- drain behavior and bounded shutdown;
- no broker notification as ownership authority.

## Backpressure and placement

Define explicit behavior for:

- selected adapter unavailable/incompatible/auth-required;
- no eligible worker/profile/placement;
- eligible worker at capacity;
- package/runtime artifact missing;
- RCC/uv cold preparation storms;
- provider outage with verified warm state;
- per-Workspace/Deployment concurrency/quotas;
- priority/starvation;
- package/runtime locality affinity as optimization;
- anti-affinity/dedicated isolation;
- platform/ABI mismatch;
- disk pressure/runtime GC;
- adapter/worker rolling upgrade;
- parent waiting on child capacity/deadlock (#147).

No capacity may yield a queued Run/Task rather than an indefinitely held protocol call. It never justifies adapter fallback.

## Physical topology

First distributed proof stays boring:

```text
Runtime A/B
   |
PostgreSQL
   |
Worker C/D
  installed adapter descriptors
  local package/runtime caches
```

No broker/Kubernetes required if DB-backed claims suffice. Prove independent processes/containers before adding platform operators.

## Required invariants

- request-handling Runtime needs no local worker/runtime for correctness;
- every Attempt executes exact pinned Package/capability/Runtime Plan/bindings/profile;
- worker cannot substitute another plan because its cache is warm;
- only current fenced owner starts/finishes/publishes;
- adapter/runtime/cache locality is optimization, never identity/authorization;
- adapter-local leases/resources live through process/output lifecycle and release after reap;
- coordinator/worker/adapter/provider/cache/client loss has explicit recovery;
- local and independent modes use same Run/Attempt/Runtime Plan semantics;
- package/runtime/provider failure cannot leave stuck ownership;
- no simulation or fallback.

## Strict acceptance matrix

1. Runtime A creates RCC Run; Worker C with RCC descriptor claims, acquires exact Environment Artifact, executes; Runtime B observes.
2. Runtime A creates uv/Robot Framework Run; compatible Worker D executes same generic contract without RCC fields.
3. selected RCC unavailable while uv is installed: worker declines, no fallback.
4. worker crashes before runtime preparation; reconciliation does not pretend code ran.
5. crash after preparation/lease before spawn cleans safely.
6. crash after spawn yields explicit receipt/ambiguous policy.
7. stale Worker C after lease loss cannot publish while D owns newer epoch.
8. cancellation through Runtime B races with package/runtime preparation/spawn/run/finish deterministically.
9. wrong adapter feature/platform/profile/grant safely declines.
10. RCC cold worker acquires prebuilt artifact through RCC; warm retry reuses verified cache.
11. provider-dead verified warm path succeeds where adapter guarantees it.
12. cold-build races publish no corrupt state.
13. GC/disk pressure preserves active adapter resources.
14. consequential retry respects effect policy.
15. drain stops new claims while active work finishes/reconciles.
16. local process-pool and independent worker pass same semantics.
17. container placement never exposes daemon socket to package code.

## Non-goals

- Agent harness/model runtime.
- General Kubernetes scheduler.
- One pod/server per package.
- Broker/RCC lock as Run fencing.
- Replacing #83 ownership.
- Reimplementing #133 or #143 inside scheduler.
- Claiming adapters/containers sandbox malicious code automatically.
- Requiring every worker to preload every runtime.
- Making every call asynchronous.
- Automatic runtime-adapter fallback.

Parent: #82
Depends on: #84, #129
Related: #86, #91, #131, #132, #137, #143

## Outcome

Replace process-global, product-specific, and adapter-specific mutable context with explicit typed authorized categories that remain correct across Runtime replicas, Runtime Adapters, and independent workers.

Do not build one distributed mutable `context` or environment-variable bag. Separate:

- request-scoped protocol metadata;
- invocation identity/authorization claims;
- immutable Deployment binding references;
- selected Runtime Plan and adapter feature identity;
- fenced Run/Attempt execution identity;
- provider-owned secret, OAuth, data, output, Work Item, package-source, and adapter-runtime handles/grants.

The same request/Run must behave when Runtime A accepts it, Worker C executes through RCC/uv/Python/Robot Framework, and Runtime B serves status/control.

## Design correction

The inherited Sema4.ai path mixed useful concerns with closed/product/process coupling:

- `ActionContext` carried secrets and invocation data;
- `DataContext`/`x-data-context` carried closed Data Server configuration;
- managed parameters resolved from headers, process environment, or product context;
- long-lived process-local values became accidental deployment state.

Historical Robot/Control Room paths also injected queue/env/runtime details directly into subprocess environments and tied execution to local RCC/package paths.

#125 removes Data Server compatibility. #129 owns immutable Deployment binding/runtime policy. #143 owns Runtime Adapter requirements/preflight. #131 owns open data/knowledge providers.

Do not recreate any of these couplings under a generic name.

## Context categories

### Request Context

Ephemeral metadata for one incoming request:

- validated allowlisted headers/cookies;
- request/correlation/trace identity;
- protocol method/name/client metadata;
- deadline, locale, bounded hints;
- authenticated actor and authorization-decision reference;
- requested capability/runtime/placement override only as untrusted input until validated.

Reconstruct per request. Never store as mutable server-global state. Only explicitly approved normalized fields flow into Run creation.

### Invocation Identity

Durable or re-resolvable audit/policy claims:

- Workspace;
- actor/service/client identity;
- authorized on-behalf-of/delegation identity;
- agent/App/Deployment identity;
- scopes/policy decision reference;
- original request/correlation identifiers;
- parent/root Run identity for internal invocation.

Caller-supplied identity headers are untrusted until auth binds them.

### Deployment Binding Snapshot

Immutable provider references/version policy resolved by #129 before Run creation:

- secret/OAuth references;
- data/knowledge connections;
- output artifact/result namespace;
- Work Item backend/queue;
- Package Revision source provider;
- adapter runtime-artifact/provider references;
- network/capability grants;
- adapter/Worker Profile policy revisions;
- retention/audit policy references.

Store references/versions, never secret plaintext or arbitrary provider URLs as identity. Deployment update affects new Runs only.

### Resolved Runtime Context

Immutable selected Runtime Plan identity from #143:

- `runtime_kind` and plan digest;
- adapter contract/version/required features;
- namespaced runtime specification/artifact policy;
- platform/architecture/ABI;
- Worker Profile/placement requirements;
- admission generation/mode/outcome used at Run creation.

This is not an activation environment or adapter execution handle.

For RCC it may reference Environment Specification/Artifact/provider profile policy. For uv/Python/Robot Framework it uses the adapter's typed plan. Generic context does not require RCC fields.

### Attempt Execution Context

Worker-visible immutable identity/grants for one fenced Attempt:

- Run/Attempt IDs;
- owner/epoch/fencing token;
- Workspace/Deployment/Package/capability/Runtime Plan;
- deadline/cancellation/effect policy;
- authorized provider handles or short-lived grants;
- output/log/result namespaces;
- optional Work Item linkage;
- parent/root Run links;
- redaction registration material/reference;
- placement/resource policy.

It is not a general key/value store and is immutable except through durable state transitions.

### Adapter-local execution material

Last-responsible-boundary derived values such as:

- RCC execution handle/env variables/lease;
- uv prepared environment/interpreter;
- Python interpreter/venv activation;
- Robot Framework runner context;
- container/session runtime receipt.

These remain worker/process-local, short-lived, fenced, and absent from durable Package/Deployment/Run/Task identity except bounded redacted provenance.

## Secret and OAuth provider contract

Define a provider seam for:

- resolving authorized references at execution time;
- version/rotation metadata without value disclosure;
- narrow/short-lived material where possible;
- redaction registration before adapter/process output;
- revocation/expiry/retry behavior;
- access audit without values;
- local env/file/keyring implementations and remote providers;
- worker-side retrieval without original Runtime affinity;
- grant invalidation after Attempt fence loss.

Provider OAuth inside a capability remains separate from MCP client authorization #91.

## Package source and runtime-artifact providers

Keep separately typed:

- **Package source provider** — immutable Package Revision bytes/manifest (#136);
- **RCC runtime provider** — Environment Artifact/object content via RCC provider profiles/`cache serve` (#133);
- **uv/other runtime providers** — adapter-specific content/cache/provider references where supported;
- **Run output provider** — logs/results/artifacts (#86).

They may reuse infrastructure but have different identities, auth, retention, and protocols.

Actions bindings carry authorized references and policy, not RCC object-index internals, provider credentials, raw URLs, local paths, or opaque untyped blobs.

`rcc cache serve` is an environment-content provider, not request/Run/worker context authority.

## Propagation rules

- every field has owner, schema, sensitivity, retention, authorization, and destination;
- unrecognized headers/context keys/env vars are not forwarded;
- large/sensitive material uses authorized handles;
- secret plaintext never enters package/runtime artifacts, Deployment/Run/Task metadata, catalogs, analytics, events, or logs;
- worker environment is deny-by-default and constructed per Attempt;
- runtime-adapter grants are least privilege and selected-plan scoped;
- one adapter cannot inherit broader grants than another simply because it is installed;
- changing runtime adapter requires new Run resolution/grant snapshot;
- serialization is versioned and negative-tested across mixed versions;
- provider/cache notifications optimize but do not authorize;
- stale/fenced Attempts cannot resolve or refresh privileged grants;
- no fallback adapter receives grants intended for a pinned adapter.

## Local mode

Local mode may source development bindings from explicit CLI flags, non-secret env files, local files, OS keyrings, or config, but compiles them into the same typed reference/grant model.

Local convenience does not permit:

- arbitrary inherited host environment;
- secret argv by default;
- package-selected provider/cache paths;
- package-provided adapter plugins;
- different managed-parameter semantics from distributed mode;
- silent RCC→Python/uv fallback with copied environment.

Development-only sources are visibly local and never copied into published Package/Deployment metadata.

## Managed capability parameters

Define an adapter-independent authoring contract for injected/managed parameters such as:

- typed request metadata;
- actor/invocation identity;
- secret/OAuth handles or last-boundary typed secret values;
- data/knowledge clients (#131);
- artifact/result handles (#86);
- Work Item context (#132);
- internal Runtime invocation client (#147);
- bounded Run/Attempt metadata where appropriate.

Managed dependencies appear in capability metadata even when omitted from public input schema.

Do not infer provider access from a Python parameter name, importable proprietary type, Robot task name, or adapter choice.

## Rotation, revocation, and pinning

Define per binding/provider class whether a pinned Run resolves:

- exact immutable version;
- current authorized version behind a stable reference;
- short-lived derived credential under pinned policy;
- or fails safely after rotation/revocation.

Record policy/evidence, never the secret.

Runtime Plan retry preserves binding snapshot semantics. Switching adapter does not bypass revocation or silently resolve newer grants.

## Required invariants

- Request Context cannot leak across calls/packages/Workspaces/workers;
- one Run pins one authorized Deployment/Runtime Plan/binding snapshot;
- workers resolve needed providers without original Runtime;
- adapter-specific material remains derived/local;
- rotation/revocation semantics are explicit;
- cross-Workspace/Deployment/provider/Runtime Plan substitution rejected;
- stale Attempts cannot use new privileged grants;
- secrets are redacted before output and absent from durable metadata;
- local/distributed and RCC/non-RCC paths expose equivalent managed-parameter semantics;
- no DataContext/x-data-context or generic mutable context store returns;
- no adapter fallback gains authority.

## Acceptance

1. Runtime A authenticates and creates a pinned capability Run with identity, Runtime Plan, and binding snapshot; Worker C resolves authorized handles and executes; Runtime B serves result.
2. The same flow passes with RCC and one non-RCC adapter without fake generic RCC fields.
3. concurrent requests prove headers/cookies/secrets/actor/provider/runtime grants cannot cross-contaminate.
4. binding rotation between Run creation/execution follows explicit tested policy.
5. Deployment update changes new Runs only.
6. cross-Workspace references, forged identity, unlisted headers/env, expired grants, stale Attempt access, and unauthorized runtime override are rejected.
7. raw/encoded secret values are absent from logs, diagnostics, Run/Task/protocol payloads, artifacts, adapter plans, and frontend state.
8. local and remote secret/provider implementations pass the same contract.
9. RCC provider profile/cache references work without copying provider credentials or object protocol into generic bindings.
10. selected adapter outage never forwards grants to a fallback adapter.
11. mixed context/runtime-plan versions fail closed on authorization-critical fields.
12. no shared mutable context store is introduced.

## Non-goals

- Recreating Data Server/DataContext.
- Storing agent memory/application state in Runtime context.
- Implementing MCP client authorization (#91).
- Giving every capability host environment or Workspace secrets.
- Treating headers/env forwarding as authorization.
- Requiring one cloud secret/OAuth/runtime provider.
- Making adapter-local activation data durable identity.
- Automatic adapter fallback.

Parent: #82
Depends on: #83, #84, #129
Related: #85, #87, #90, #131, #132, #137, #143

## Outcome

Replace the assumption that Run artifacts, logs, screenshots, reports, Robot Framework outputs, generated bundles, files, and large results live only on the local Runtime/worker filesystem with an explicit replica-safe storage and metadata contract.

An artifact produced by Worker C through RCC, uv, Python, Robot Framework, or another adapter must be discoverable/authorized through Runtime B without routing to the producer process/node. A stale Attempt cannot finalize authoritative output after fence loss.

## Artifact planes remain separate

Do not conflate:

- Package Revision source artifacts (#136);
- adapter runtime artifacts/providers:
  - RCC Environment Artifacts/provider content (#133);
  - uv/other immutable runtime content where supported;
- Run output artifacts/results owned here;
- data/knowledge result handles (#131).

They may reuse storage/CAS infrastructure but have different identity, auth, retention, publication, and GC semantics.

`rcc cache serve` distributes RCC runtime content, not Run screenshots/reports/logs unless a separately typed output provider explicitly implements both contracts.

## Artifact metadata

Durable metadata through #84 includes:

- Workspace;
- Deployment Revision;
- Package Revision/capability/schema;
- selected Runtime Plan/`runtime_kind`/plan digest;
- Run/Attempt/owner/epoch;
- Worker Profile/placement and bounded execution receipt reference;
- optional Workflow/Node/Work Item/parent-child lineage;
- artifact ID/content digest;
- logical name/media type/size/schema/type/purpose;
- storage provider/opaque object reference;
- provisional/final/aborted/expired state;
- creation/finalization/expiry;
- retention/legal hold/encryption/access policy;
- producer capability/adapter and related result/Task handles;
- redaction/truncation/validation provenance.

Adapter-local paths, RCC/venv/interpreter paths, container IDs, provider URLs, and credentials are not portable artifact identity.

## Blob/object provider contract

Support at least:

- local filesystem for collapsed development;
- one shared/object implementation for independent replicas;
- bounded streaming upload/download;
- atomic/two-phase provisional→final publish;
- abort/cleanup of incomplete uploads;
- range reads;
- optional short-lived provider-native signed access under policy;
- checksum/digest verification;
- safe content-addressed reuse;
- metadata/list/head/delete;
- typed health/failure behavior;
- immutable finalized bytes unless separate versioned mutable-resource contract exists.

Database stores metadata/opaque references, not large blobs except explicit local/small-object policy.

Do not expose arbitrary host paths, bucket keys, raw provider credentials, or unmanaged signed URLs as durable client contract.

## Publication lifecycle

A producer Attempt:

1. creates provisional metadata tied to Run/Attempt/current epoch and selected Runtime Plan;
2. streams bytes to authorized output provider;
3. enforces per-artifact/Run limits and content policy;
4. verifies size/digest and closes upload;
5. conditionally finalizes under current fence;
6. transactionally attaches final reference to Run/Workflow/Work Item result;
7. leaves failed/stale uploads abortable/GC-able;
8. releases adapter/process resources only after required publication completes.

If owner loses fence before finalization, bytes may remain temporarily but cannot become authoritative output. Cleanup must preserve content referenced by another finalized artifact safely.

## Adapter-native output collection

#143 adapters declare output behavior.

### RCC

Collect declared `artifactsDir`, Robot/Action outputs, screenshots, reports, stdout/stderr, and structured receipt through supported RCC/runtime execution lifecycle. RCC Environment Artifact content remains a separate runtime-artifact plane.

### Robot Framework

Collect output XML, log/report HTML, screenshots, listener outputs, and declared files safely.

### uv/Python

Collect declared output roots/files and structured results without scanning arbitrary host filesystem.

Common rules:

- package declares allowed output roots/patterns/limits;
- adapter/worker does not publish arbitrary host files;
- symlink/path escape/special files rejected;
- secrets redacted where known before durable publication;
- binary/sensitive output treated under explicit policy;
- adapter unavailable/failure never synthesizes artifact/result success.

## Logs/events/results

Define relationship among:

- best-effort live events/log streaming;
- durable ordered log chunks/streams;
- final log artifacts;
- compact Task/protocol progress;
- structured inline result versus large-result handle.

Durable state/result never depends on original WebSocket/SSE/MCP request/worker process.

Logs define ordering/cursors, truncation/rate limits, binary handling, multiline/log-injection protection, redaction, retention, and stale-owner publication rejection.

## Identity and naming

- Artifact IDs globally unambiguous or explicitly Workspace-scoped.
- Logical names are presentation metadata, never storage paths.
- Content digest identifies bytes, not authorization.
- Same bytes may back multiple artifact metadata records with separate lineage/policy.
- User uploads, Package build outputs, Runtime Adapter content, Run outputs, logs, screenshots, datasets, and reports are typed distinctly.
- Artifact metadata records exact producing Runtime Plan without requiring generic RCC fields.

## Authorization/security

- every read/list/export/signed-access/delete authorized by Workspace/Deployment/actor/policy;
- provider credentials never returned to clients/package code unless explicit narrow capability grants direct access;
- signed URLs short-lived/scoped/audited/HTTPS/revocable;
- upload names/archives protected against traversal, symlinks, bombs, type confusion, expansion;
- artifact content treated as potentially sensitive;
- encryption/KMS references are provider policy, not package secrets;
- cross-Workspace dedupe cannot leak existence/timing;
- stale Attempt/adapter process cannot publish after fence loss;
- package cannot redirect output root to provider/cache/runtime directories;
- selected adapter cannot receive broader output-store grants than policy.

## Retention/GC

Coordinate retention for:

- Run/Attempt/Task;
- Workflow/Work Item/review evidence;
- artifact metadata/object bytes;
- large data/result handles;
- Package/Canvas build outputs;
- adapter execution receipts.

Keep Package source and adapter runtime artifacts separately referenced/retained.

GC handles provisional/abandoned multipart, expired handles, deleted Runs, shared content, legal holds, provider failure, and referenced historical evidence. A successful Task/Run cannot claim available output after required object removal; unavailable/expired state explicit.

## Local mode

Local filesystem provider uses Runtime-owned root and opaque IDs while enforcing same path safety, provisional/final state, fencing, auth, retention, and Runtime Plan lineage.

Do not return absolute paths as portable identity.

## Required invariants

- artifact/result identity independent of producing Runtime/worker/adapter-local path;
- only current fenced Attempt finalizes authoritative output;
- final metadata never points to incomplete/unverified bytes;
- finalized artifact immutable;
- another Runtime replica returns same authorized bytes/metadata;
- digest equality never bypasses auth/lineage;
- provider outage/partial upload cannot falsely complete Run;
- selected Runtime Plan is preserved in output provenance;
- local/shared backends pass same core suite;
- Runtime artifact and Run output planes remain distinct.

## Acceptance

1. Worker C using RCC finalizes artifact; Runtime B lists/reads with Deployment/Package/capability/Runtime Plan/Run/Attempt lineage.
2. One non-RCC adapter publishes through same generic contract without fake RCC fields.
3. fence loss before finalize rejects publication and later cleans provisional bytes.
4. duplicate content reuse does not merge authorization metadata.
5. interrupted/checksum-mismatch/oversized/traversal/symlink/bomb/type-confusion outputs fail safely.
6. range/streaming reads use bounded memory.
7. cross-Workspace/forged/expired/deleted references rejected.
8. retention/GC handles shared/provisional/legal hold/provider outage without dangling false success.
9. local filesystem and one shared provider pass same suite.
10. frozen/source/wheel installations resolve safe local roots consistently.
11. large data/export uses handles rather than model context.
12. RCC `cache serve` runtime-content provider and output artifact provider remain separately typed/evidenced.
13. adapter failure/simulation cannot produce authoritative result artifact.

## Non-goals

- Tabular query API (#131).
- Storing all bytes in Runtime DB.
- Unrestricted bucket/filesystem access for package code.
- Live WebSocket/SSE as durable log store.
- Requiring S3/cloud provider.
- Content addressing as authorization.
- Using Run output store as RCC/uv runtime artifact protocol automatically.

Parent: #82
Depends on: #80, #83, #84, #129
Related: #86, #90, #92, #132, #137, #143

## Outcome

Map MCP Tasks onto the durable **capability Run** model instead of creating a parallel async-job subsystem.

An MCP Task is the protocol-facing authorized handle for one long-running, queued, disconnected, or paused Run. It is not a worker Attempt, Work Item, runtime-adapter job, cache/provider request, placement receipt, transport session, or new source of execution truth.

The same Task model applies to long-running Actions, Robot Tasks, Workflows, and other projected capabilities.

## Object mapping

Keep boundaries explicit:

- **Run:** one durable logical execution pinned to Workspace/Deployment Revision, Package Revision, capability identity/schema, selected Runtime Plan/`runtime_kind`, binding/policy snapshot, Worker Profile/placement constraints, and normalized input.
- **Attempt:** one fenced worker try owned by #83/#90 and executed through the pinned adapter/placement.
- **MCP Task:** authorized protocol projection of the Run's status, result, control, expiry, interactions, and related resources.
- **Work Item:** durable business/queue/review lifecycle owned by #132; it may initiate or aggregate Runs but is not automatically a Task.
- **Adapter execution handle:** worker-local/process-local control evidence, never the public Task identity.

Use `run_id` as the durable backing identity or a justified one-to-one mapping. Do not put MCP session, Runtime replica, worker, PID/container ID, RCC/uv/venv path, provider URL, or adapter-local lease into Task identity.

## When to return a Task

Define deterministic policy for:

- capabilities declared long-running;
- requests exceeding bounded synchronous budget;
- queued Runs awaiting runtime preparation or worker capacity;
- detached CLI/UI/internal invocation;
- interactive/review-paused Runs;
- clients explicitly requesting Task behavior where protocol allows it.

Bounded local calls may still complete synchronously. Do not turn every capability call into polling.

## Required Task surface

Map MCP Task operations to durable Run semantics:

- creation acknowledgement and Task/Run identity;
- current state, phase, timestamps, and compact progress;
- selected capability and Runtime Plan summary without local paths/secrets;
- blocked/waiting reason such as runtime preparation, auth-required provider, or no eligible worker;
- result/resource references;
- structured failure/cancellation/ambiguous/review information;
- cancellation intent and final durable outcome;
- expiry/retention/unavailable/garbage-collected behavior;
- authorized logs, artifacts, large-result handles, Work Item links, child Runs, and review handles;
- request correlation and idempotent Task/Run creation retry.

Task payloads contain compact receipts and handles, not unbounded logs/files/tables or adapter internals.

## State mapping

Document/test mapping from rich Run states to protocol Task states while preserving distinctions such as:

- queued;
- waiting for package/runtime preparation;
- waiting for eligible worker/capacity;
- executing;
- retrying after Attempt loss;
- paused/awaiting interaction/review;
- cancellation requested versus cancelled;
- succeeded;
- business failure;
- application/infrastructure failure;
- terminal ambiguous/manual review;
- expired/result unavailable.

A new Attempt does not reset Task identity. A new adapter is not an Attempt retry; changing Runtime Plan requires a new Run/Task resolution.

## Runtime Plan pinning and no fallback

Task creation exposes/retains the Run's exact selected Runtime Plan.

- Deployment update/rollback affects new Runs only.
- Attempt retry preserves Package, capability, adapter, runtime specification/artifact policy, bindings, and Worker Profile.
- a Task pinned to RCC does not silently become uv/Python/Robot Framework when RCC is unavailable;
- compatible alternatives may be shown as information only when declared, but selecting one creates a deliberate new Run;
- Task status distinguishes unavailable/auth-required/incompatible/no-worker from execution failure;
- simulation/synthetic success is prohibited.

RCC Environment Artifact identity may appear as namespaced evidence for an RCC plan. It is not mandatory generic Task schema.

## Authorization

- Task read/control authorization is Workspace-scoped and rechecked on every request;
- related result/artifact/data/Work Item/interaction handles have their own authorization and retention;
- Deployment/provider references are redacted/bounded;
- provider credentials, secrets, local runtime paths, worker leases, and adapter handles never appear;
- cross-Workspace Task IDs do not grant existence/read/control.

## Cancellation and races

Task cancellation writes durable Run cancellation intent through #83.

Cover races with:

- idempotent creation;
- queued assignment;
- package fetch;
- RCC/uv/other runtime preparation;
- Attempt claim/spawn;
- external effects;
- worker finish;
- Attempt retry;
- interaction/review;
- result/artifact publication;
- Task expiry.

Protocol response reflects durable state-machine outcome, not merely acceptance by one Runtime or signal delivery to one process.

## Interactive flows

#92 may use MRTR for connected interaction, but Run/Task/interaction state survives disconnect. Task status exposes a stable authorized review/interaction handle and compact reason. Resumption may create a new Attempt while preserving Task identity and Runtime Plan.

## Work Items and workflows

#132 owns Work Item business state; #141 owns Workflow Runs/nodes.

- Task cancellation/completion does not silently rewrite Work Item outcome;
- one Workflow Run may expose a Task while child capability Runs retain their own identities;
- parent/child Task/Run relationships use compact links;
- Work Item→Workflow/Run→Attempt lineage remains explicit and fenced.

## Required invariants

- one Task survives reconnect, Runtime replica changes, Attempt replacement, and worker restart;
- status/result/control derive from durable Run state;
- exact Package/capability/Runtime Plan remains pinned;
- no adapter fallback or simulation;
- idempotent retries do not duplicate consequential Runs;
- no sticky routing or shared transport-session store;
- stale worker/Attempt cannot publish result/artifacts;
- Task/Run/artifact retention cannot report false success after required output is gone.

## Acceptance

1. A long-running Action returns one Task backed by a pinned capability Run.
2. A long-running Robot Task uses the same Task model and shows its selected runtime adapter without RCC-only generic fields.
3. Status/result survive disconnect and another Runtime replica serves them.
4. Worker loss creates a new Attempt while preserving Task identity/Runtime Plan and visible retry phase.
5. Cancellation through another Runtime is durable and race-tested across preparation, queue, spawn, finish, and publication.
6. Deployment update does not change code, runtime adapter, bindings, or catalog identity of an existing Task.
7. An RCC-pinned Task never falls back despite a compatible uv adapter being installed.
8. Large results/logs/artifacts use handles.
9. authorization, expiry, GC, idempotency, and cross-Workspace negatives pass.
10. bounded synchronous calls remain simple.
11. no parallel Task database or adapter-owned async state machine is introduced.

## Non-goals

- Using MCP Tasks as worker queue.
- Replacing Work Items or Workflow state.
- Exposing Attempt/adapter handles as primary client identity.
- Holding MCP connection for Run lifetime.
- Claiming cancellation undoes committed external effects.
- Changing runtime adapter as an infrastructure retry.

Parent: #82
Blocks: #83, #85, #90, #129, #132
Implementation lane: #109

## Outcome

Provide one repository-owned `Database` abstraction that supports:

- SQLite as the default local, single-binary backend;
- PostgreSQL as the shared production backend for independent Runtime and worker processes;
- one migration history and one externally visible persistence contract across both backends.

This issue owns database connectivity, SQL execution/translation boundaries, transactions, migration coordination, data preservation, packaging, diagnostics, and backend parity. It does not own Run leases, worker scheduling, Work Item lifecycle, or Deployment semantics; those must build on this abstraction in #83, #90, #129, and #132 rather than creating another database layer.

## Current implementation direction

PR #109 is the active implementation lane. It introduces the shared database seam and PostgreSQL support while preserving SQLite. The final merged contract must be reviewed against this issue, not treated as complete merely because a PostgreSQL URL can connect.

Required public capabilities include, at minimum:

- explicit `backend_name` or equivalent backend capability inspection;
- connection lifecycle with SQLite and PostgreSQL URL validation;
- transaction context with commit/rollback semantics;
- query/execute and update row-count behavior;
- update-and-returning support needed for atomic claims and fencing;
- safe parameter adaptation without rewriting quoted/comment/dollar-quoted SQL incorrectly;
- backend-time access for lease and scheduling correctness;
- coordinated schema migration startup;
- consistent row/result mapping and typed error boundaries.

Use this one abstraction for all Runtime control-plane persistence. Do not add a separate ownership, queue, Deployment, or Canvas database wrapper.

## Backend semantics

### SQLite

- remains the default for local use;
- preserves current data-directory and backup/migration behavior;
- uses transactions and locking appropriate to one or a small number of local processes;
- documents topology limits honestly rather than pretending a filesystem SQLite file is a general multi-node database.

### PostgreSQL

- supports multiple independent Runtime/worker processes against one database;
- uses PostgreSQL-native transactions, booleans, locking, and `RETURNING` semantics where required;
- handles connection loss, retries at safe boundaries, statement errors, and rollback without partial state;
- supports SSL/query options without leaking credentials or sensitive URL components;
- works in source, wheel, and frozen-binary installations.

Backend-specific SQL is allowed behind the abstraction where correctness requires it. Do not force both engines through a lowest-common-denominator string-rewrite layer that weakens transactional semantics.

## Migration contract

- One monotonically ordered migration history covers both backends.
- Startup coordination prevents two replicas from applying the same migration concurrently.
- PostgreSQL may use transaction-scoped advisory locking or an equivalent database-native mechanism.
- SQLite retains an appropriate local lock/transaction mechanism.
- A migration either commits its complete intended result or rolls back without dropping distinct source data.
- Existing rows, null/partial legacy data, archive records, indexes, constraints, and foreign-key relationships have explicit preservation tests.
- Re-running an applied migration is idempotent or rejected safely.
- A newer schema is not silently opened by an incompatible older Runtime.
- Backup/archive/recovery behavior is documented and exercised before destructive migration steps.

Migration acceptance must include collision cases, not only fresh empty databases.

## Security and diagnostics

- Database URLs are redacted everywhere they may appear: startup settings, CLI errors, migration diagnostics, logs, exceptions, analytics, and test failure output.
- Redaction removes username, password, query, fragment, percent-encoded forms, and other credential-bearing material while retaining enough endpoint context to diagnose safely.
- The live connection value is not mutated by logging redaction.
- SQL parameter values containing secrets are not logged by default.
- TLS/SSL configuration and certificate behavior are explicit for PostgreSQL.
- Database credentials never enter package manifests, MCP catalogs, Run results, or frontend state.

## Transaction and concurrency requirements

The abstraction must be sufficient for later strict contracts:

- atomic idempotent Run creation;
- owner/lease/epoch claims using database time;
- conditional heartbeat and terminal updates;
- finish-versus-cancel arbitration;
- Deployment revision swaps and pinning;
- Work Item claims, review transitions, and output publication;
- artifact/result metadata commits.

This does not implement those features here, but the shared database API must not make them impossible or require bypassing the abstraction.

Define behavior for:

- nested transaction attempts;
- write conflicts and serialization/deadlock retry boundaries;
- affected-row checks for conditional transitions;
- read-after-write consistency;
- connection pooling versus per-operation connections;
- long-running transactions and shutdown;
- database unavailable at startup and during requests;
- migration lock timeout and operator recovery.

## Required invariants

- SQLite and PostgreSQL expose equivalent externally visible Runtime state for the same operation sequence.
- A failed transaction cannot leave a partially authoritative Run, Deployment, Work Item, result, or migration state.
- Conditional updates report whether the expected state/owner/epoch actually matched.
- Database/provider time is available for correctness-sensitive leases and schedules.
- Migration coordination is database-backed and does not depend on one elected Runtime process.
- No code path silently falls back from a configured PostgreSQL database to local SQLite.
- Source, wheel, and frozen binary choose the same backend for the same validated configuration.
- Backend selection, URL redaction, and driver loading work on supported platforms.

## Acceptance matrix

- [ ] Two independent Runtime processes connect to and mutate the same PostgreSQL database safely.
- [ ] SQLite remains the zero-configuration local default and passes the shared behavioral suite.
- [ ] Concurrent migration startup applies each migration once on SQLite and PostgreSQL.
- [ ] Fresh, legacy, partial/null, equal-collision, unequal-collision, rollback, retry-after-operator-resolution, and rerun migration cases preserve data.
- [ ] Transactions, rollback, affected-row checks, and update-returning behavior are covered on both backends.
- [ ] SQL parameter/operator adaptation is tested across strings, identifiers, comments, dollar-quoted blocks, JSON operators, arrays, casts, and placeholder-count mismatches.
- [ ] Connection loss and database-unavailable behavior fail clearly without partial authoritative state or hidden fallback.
- [ ] URL/credential redaction has negative tests for raw and encoded credentials, query tokens, and fragments across every diagnostic path.
- [ ] Wheel, sdist, clean install, PyInstaller/frozen binary, migration CLI, and server startup work with SQLite and PostgreSQL.
- [ ] Mixed Runtime/schema versions fail safely and document the supported rolling-upgrade window.
- [ ] #83 can implement DB-time lease/epoch fencing entirely through this abstraction without raw backend access or a second database layer.

## Non-goals

- Implementing the Run/Attempt ownership state machine; #83 owns it.
- Implementing workers or scheduling; #90 owns them.
- Implementing Work Item business lifecycle; #132 owns it.
- Becoming an ORM or general application database toolkit.
- Removing SQLite or requiring PostgreSQL for local development.
- Supporting every SQL database before the SQLite/PostgreSQL contract is proven.

